/* AI brain Q&A pack 19: biology, plants, chemistry & physics */
(window.AIQA = window.AIQA || []).push(
  ["what is cell theory | the three parts of cell theory | cell theory explained", "**Cell theory** says three things: all living things are made of one or more cells, the cell is the basic unit of life, and all cells come from existing cells. It was worked out in the 1800s by Schleiden, Schwann and Virchow."],
  ["who discovered cells | who first saw cells | robert hooke cells", "**Robert Hooke** first described \"cells\" in 1665 after looking at thin slices of cork under a microscope. A few years later, Antonie van Leeuwenhoek saw living single-celled organisms with his own handmade microscopes."],
  ["what does the nucleus do | function of the nucleus | nucleus of a cell", "The **nucleus** is the cell's control center. It holds the DNA (the instructions for making proteins) and is wrapped in a double membrane called the nuclear envelope."],
  ["what is the nucleolus | what does the nucleolus do", "The **nucleolus** is a dense spot inside the nucleus where ribosomes are put together. It makes ribosomal RNA and combines it with proteins before sending the parts out to the cytoplasm."],
  ["what does the cell membrane do | function of the cell membrane | what is the plasma membrane", "The **cell membrane** is a thin, flexible layer of fats (a phospholipid bilayer) and proteins around every cell. It controls what goes in and out, so it's described as *selectively permeable*."],
  ["what does the cell wall do | function of the cell wall | what is a cell wall made of", "The **cell wall** is a stiff layer outside the cell membrane that gives support and protection. In plants it's made of cellulose, in fungi of chitin, and bacteria have their own type made of peptidoglycan. Animal cells don't have one."],
  ["what is cytoplasm | what does the cytoplasm do", "**Cytoplasm** is the jelly-like fluid that fills the cell and surrounds the organelles. Many chemical reactions happen in it, and it helps hold everything in place."],
  ["what do ribosomes do | function of ribosomes | what are ribosomes", "**Ribosomes** are tiny structures that build proteins by reading instructions from messenger RNA and linking amino acids together. They float free in the cytoplasm or sit on the rough endoplasmic reticulum."],
  ["what is the endoplasmic reticulum | what does the er do | rough er vs smooth er", "The **endoplasmic reticulum (ER)** is a network of folded membranes. **Rough ER** is studded with ribosomes and helps make and fold proteins, while **smooth ER** makes lipids (fats) and helps break down toxins."],
  ["what does the golgi apparatus do | function of the golgi body | what is the golgi", "The **Golgi apparatus** is like the cell's post office. It receives proteins and lipids from the ER, modifies and packages them into little bubbles called vesicles, and ships them where they're needed."],
  ["what do lysosomes do | function of lysosomes | what are lysosomes", "**Lysosomes** are small sacs full of digestive enzymes. They break down worn-out cell parts, food particles and invading germs, acting as the cell's recycling center. They're mostly found in animal cells."],
  ["what does the vacuole do | function of the vacuole | why do plant cells have a big vacuole", "A **vacuole** is a storage sac for water, food and waste. Plant cells have one large central vacuole that pushes against the cell wall to keep the plant firm; when it loses water, the plant wilts."],
  ["what do chloroplasts do | function of chloroplasts | what are chloroplasts", "**Chloroplasts** are the organelles where photosynthesis happens. They contain green chlorophyll that captures sunlight to turn carbon dioxide and water into glucose. Plant and algae cells have them, animal cells don't."],
  ["why do mitochondria have their own dna | where did mitochondria come from | endosymbiotic theory", "Mitochondria (and chloroplasts) have their own DNA because they were probably once free-living bacteria that got swallowed by a larger cell and stayed to work as partners. This idea is called the **endosymbiotic theory**."],
  ["what is the cytoskeleton | what does the cytoskeleton do", "The **cytoskeleton** is a network of protein fibers (microfilaments, microtubules and intermediate filaments) that gives the cell its shape, helps it move and acts as tracks for moving things around inside."],
  ["what are centrioles | what do centrioles do", "**Centrioles** are small tube-shaped structures found in animal cells, usually in pairs near the nucleus. They help organize the spindle fibers that pull chromosomes apart during cell division."],
  ["what are vesicles | what does a vesicle do in a cell", "**Vesicles** are tiny bubbles of membrane that carry materials around the cell, such as proteins from the Golgi to the cell membrane. Some release their contents outside the cell."],
  ["what are cilia and flagella | what is a flagellum | what do cilia do", "**Cilia** are short hair-like structures and **flagella** are long whip-like tails that stick out from some cells. Flagella help cells like sperm and some bacteria swim, while cilia wave to move fluid, like the ones clearing mucus from your airways."],
  ["what is a prokaryote | what are prokaryotic cells", "A **prokaryote** is a simple single-celled organism with no nucleus and no membrane-bound organelles; its DNA floats in the cytoplasm. Bacteria and archaea are prokaryotes."],
  ["what is a eukaryote | what are eukaryotic cells", "A **eukaryote** is an organism whose cells have a nucleus and membrane-bound organelles like mitochondria. Animals, plants, fungi and protists are all eukaryotes."],
  ["prokaryotic vs eukaryotic cells | difference between prokaryotes and eukaryotes", "**Prokaryotic** cells (bacteria, archaea) are small and simple with no nucleus. **Eukaryotic** cells (animals, plants, fungi, protists) are larger, have a nucleus holding their DNA and contain organelles. Both have a cell membrane, cytoplasm, ribosomes and DNA."],
  ["are bacteria prokaryotes or eukaryotes | is a bacterium a eukaryote", "Bacteria are **prokaryotes**. They have no nucleus, and their DNA is usually one circular chromosome floating in the cytoplasm, sometimes with extra small loops called plasmids."],
  ["what is the smallest unit of life | what is the basic unit of life", "The **cell** is the smallest unit that can be considered alive. Anything smaller, like a single organelle or a molecule, can't live on its own."],
  ["what is the largest cell | what is the biggest single cell", "An **ostrich egg yolk** is often called the largest single cell. In the human body, the egg cell (ovum) is the largest, and some nerve cells are the longest, reaching about a meter."],
  ["what is a unicellular organism | single celled vs multicellular | what does multicellular mean", "A **unicellular** organism is made of just one cell, like bacteria, amoebas and yeast. A **multicellular** organism, like you, a tree or a mushroom, is made of many cells that specialize in different jobs."],
  ["what are the levels of organization in biology | cells tissues organs organ systems", "Living things are organized from small to big: **cells** form **tissues**, tissues form **organs**, organs work together in **organ systems**, and organ systems make up an **organism**."],
  ["what is a tissue | what are the four types of tissue", "A **tissue** is a group of similar cells doing the same job. Animals have four main types: epithelial (covering), connective (support, like bone and blood), muscle and nervous tissue."],
  ["what is diffusion | how does diffusion work in cells", "**Diffusion** is the movement of particles from an area of high concentration to low concentration until they're spread out evenly. Oxygen moves into your cells this way, and it doesn't need any energy."],
  ["what is osmosis | how does osmosis work", "**Osmosis** is the diffusion of water across a semi-permeable membrane, from where there's more water (less dissolved stuff) to where there's less water (more dissolved stuff). It's why a raisin swells when soaked in water."],
  ["what is active transport | active vs passive transport", "**Passive transport** (like diffusion and osmosis) moves things from high to low concentration with no energy needed. **Active transport** uses energy (ATP) to pump things against the gradient, from low to high concentration."],
  ["what is atp | what does atp stand for", "**ATP** stands for adenosine triphosphate. It's the energy \"currency\" of cells: when a phosphate group is snapped off, energy is released to power muscle movement, active transport and building molecules."],
  ["what are stem cells | why are stem cells important", "**Stem cells** are unspecialized cells that can divide and turn into different types of cells, like blood, muscle or nerve cells. They help the body grow and repair itself, and scientists study them for medical treatments."],
  ["what is cell differentiation | how do cells specialize", "**Differentiation** is when a cell becomes specialized for a particular job, such as a red blood cell or a neuron. All cells have the same DNA, but each type switches on different genes."],
  ["what is an enzyme | what do enzymes do", "**Enzymes** are proteins that speed up chemical reactions in living things without being used up. Each one fits its target molecule like a lock and key, for example amylase in your saliva breaks starch into sugar."],
  ["what is a protein | what are proteins made of", "**Proteins** are large molecules made of chains of **amino acids** folded into specific shapes. They build muscles, act as enzymes, carry oxygen (hemoglobin) and do countless other jobs in your body."],
  ["what are the four main biomolecules | what are macromolecules | carbs lipids proteins nucleic acids", "The four main molecules of life are **carbohydrates** (energy), **lipids** (fats for energy storage and membranes), **proteins** (structure and enzymes) and **nucleic acids** (DNA and RNA for genetic information)."],
  ["what is rna | difference between dna and rna | dna vs rna", "**RNA** is a molecule similar to DNA that helps turn genetic instructions into proteins. Unlike DNA, it's usually single-stranded, uses the sugar ribose, and has the base uracil (U) instead of thymine (T)."],
  ["what is protein synthesis | how are proteins made | transcription and translation", "Protein synthesis has two steps. In **transcription**, a gene's DNA is copied into messenger RNA in the nucleus; in **translation**, ribosomes read the mRNA three bases at a time and join the matching amino acids into a protein."],
  ["what are base pairs | how do dna bases pair up | a pairs with t", "In DNA, **adenine (A) pairs with thymine (T)** and **cytosine (C) pairs with guanine (G)**. These base pairs form the rungs of the twisted ladder (double helix)."],
  ["how does dna copy itself | what is dna replication", "In **DNA replication**, the double helix unzips down the middle and each strand acts as a template. New matching bases are added to each side, making two identical DNA molecules, each with one old strand and one new."],
  ["what is the human genome | how many genes do humans have", "The **human genome** is the complete set of DNA in a human cell, about 3 billion base pairs. It contains roughly **20,000 genes** that code for proteins, which is much less than scientists once expected."],
  ["what is the cell cycle | stages of the cell cycle", "The **cell cycle** is the life of a cell from one division to the next. It has **interphase** (the cell grows and copies its DNA) and the **mitotic phase** (mitosis plus cytokinesis, where it divides into two)."],
  ["what is interphase | what happens during interphase", "**Interphase** is the longest part of the cell cycle. The cell grows (G1), copies all its DNA (S phase) and prepares for division (G2). Most cells spend most of their time here."],
  ["what is mitosis | what happens in mitosis | why do cells do mitosis", "**Mitosis** is cell division that makes two identical daughter cells, each with the same number of chromosomes as the parent. It's used for growth, repairing damage and replacing old cells."],
  ["what are the stages of mitosis | pmat | prophase metaphase anaphase telophase", "The stages of mitosis are **PMAT**: **prophase** (chromosomes condense), **metaphase** (they line up in the middle), **anaphase** (sister chromatids are pulled apart) and **telophase** (two new nuclei form). Cytokinesis then splits the cell."],
  ["what happens in prophase | prophase explained", "In **prophase**, the chromosomes coil up and become visible, the nuclear envelope breaks down, and spindle fibers start to form from opposite ends of the cell."],
  ["what happens in metaphase | metaphase explained", "In **metaphase**, the chromosomes line up single file across the middle of the cell (the metaphase plate), with spindle fibers attached to each one."],
  ["what happens in anaphase | anaphase explained", "In **anaphase**, the spindle fibers shorten and pull the sister chromatids of each chromosome apart toward opposite ends of the cell."],
  ["what happens in telophase | telophase explained", "In **telophase**, the separated chromosomes reach the ends of the cell, start to uncoil, and a new nuclear envelope forms around each set, giving two nuclei."],
  ["what is cytokinesis | how does the cell split | cytokinesis in plants vs animals", "**Cytokinesis** is the splitting of the cytoplasm into two cells. In animal cells the membrane pinches in (a cleavage furrow); in plant cells a new **cell plate** forms down the middle and becomes a cell wall."],
  ["what is meiosis | why is meiosis important", "**Meiosis** is a special cell division that makes sex cells (sperm and eggs). One cell divides twice to make **four cells**, each with **half** the normal number of chromosomes, and each genetically different."],
  ["mitosis vs meiosis | difference between mitosis and meiosis", "**Mitosis** makes 2 identical cells with the full chromosome number, for growth and repair. **Meiosis** makes 4 different cells with half the chromosomes, for making sex cells. Mitosis has one division; meiosis has two."],
  ["what is crossing over | crossing over in meiosis", "**Crossing over** happens in meiosis when matching chromosomes swap pieces of DNA. It mixes up genes so every egg or sperm is unique, which is a big source of genetic variety."],
  ["what are gametes | what are sex cells", "**Gametes** are sex cells: **sperm** in males and **eggs** (ova) in females. In humans each has 23 chromosomes, and when they join at fertilization they form a zygote with 46."],
  ["what is haploid vs diploid | what does diploid mean | what does haploid mean", "**Diploid** cells have two sets of chromosomes (46 in humans), one from each parent. **Haploid** cells, like sperm and eggs, have only one set (23 in humans)."],
  ["what is fertilization | what is a zygote", "**Fertilization** is when a sperm and an egg fuse. The resulting single cell, the **zygote**, has a full set of chromosomes and begins dividing to grow into a new organism."],
  ["what is cancer in terms of cells | why does cancer happen", "Cancer happens when cells grow and divide out of control because of changes (mutations) in genes that normally regulate the cell cycle. These cells can form lumps called tumors and sometimes spread."],
  ["what is a chromatid | what are sister chromatids", "A **chromatid** is one of two identical copies of a chromosome made when DNA is copied. The two **sister chromatids** are joined at the centromere until they're pulled apart during cell division."],
  ["what is an allele | what are alleles", "An **allele** is a version of a gene. For example, the gene for pea flower color has a purple allele and a white allele. You get one allele for each gene from each parent."],
  ["what is a dominant allele | what does dominant mean in genetics", "A **dominant** allele shows its trait even if you have only one copy of it. It's written with a capital letter, like **B** for brown eyes."],
  ["what is a recessive allele | what does recessive mean in genetics", "A **recessive** allele only shows its trait if you have two copies of it (one from each parent). It's written with a lowercase letter, like **b**. If a dominant allele is present, it hides the recessive one."],
  ["what is genotype vs phenotype | difference between genotype and phenotype", "Your **genotype** is the set of alleles you have (like Bb). Your **phenotype** is the trait you can actually observe (like brown eyes). Environment can also affect phenotype."],
  ["what does homozygous mean | what does heterozygous mean | homozygous vs heterozygous", "**Homozygous** means having two identical alleles for a gene (BB or bb). **Heterozygous** means having two different alleles (Bb)."],
  ["what is a punnett square | how do you do a punnett square", "A **Punnett square** is a grid that predicts the possible offspring of two parents. Put one parent's alleles across the top and the other's down the side, then fill each box with one letter from each to see the possible genotypes."],
  ["what is the ratio for bb x bb | heterozygous cross ratio | 3 to 1 ratio genetics", "Crossing two heterozygous parents (**Bb x Bb**) gives genotypes in a **1 BB : 2 Bb : 1 bb** ratio. With a dominant trait, that's a **3 : 1** phenotype ratio, so there's a 25% chance of the recessive trait."],
  ["what is a monohybrid cross | what is a dihybrid cross", "A **monohybrid cross** follows one gene (like flower color), and a **dihybrid cross** follows two genes at once (like seed color and seed shape). A dihybrid cross of two heterozygotes gives a classic **9:3:3:1** ratio."],
  ["what is incomplete dominance | what is codominance", "In **incomplete dominance**, the alleles blend, like red and white snapdragons making pink flowers. In **codominance**, both show fully at once, like AB blood type or a cow with both red and white hairs."],
  ["how are blood types inherited | blood type genetics", "Blood type is controlled by one gene with three alleles: **A**, **B** and **O**. A and B are codominant and O is recessive, so AO gives type A, BO gives type B, AB gives type AB and OO gives type O."],
  ["what decides if a baby is a boy or girl | what are sex chromosomes | xx and xy", "Humans have one pair of sex chromosomes: typically **XX** for female and **XY** for male. The egg always carries an X, so whether the sperm carries an X or a Y usually decides the baby's sex."],
  ["what is a sex linked trait | why are boys more likely to be colorblind", "A **sex-linked trait** is controlled by a gene on the X chromosome. Since males have only one X, a single recessive allele (like for red-green colorblindness) will show up, while females would need two copies."],
  ["what is a mutation | what causes mutations", "A **mutation** is a change in the DNA sequence. It can happen by accident when DNA is copied, or be caused by things like UV light, radiation or certain chemicals. Most mutations are harmless, some are harmful and a few are helpful."],
  ["are all mutations bad | can mutations be good", "No! Most mutations have no effect, and some are helpful, like ones that let some adults digest milk. Helpful mutations are the raw material that natural selection works with."],
  ["what are the types of mutations | point mutation vs frameshift", "A **point mutation** changes one base (a substitution). Adding or deleting bases can cause a **frameshift**, which scrambles all the three-letter codons after it. Larger mutations can delete, duplicate or flip whole chunks of chromosomes."],
  ["what is a genetic disorder | examples of genetic disorders", "A **genetic disorder** is a condition caused by changes in genes or chromosomes. Examples include cystic fibrosis and sickle cell disease (recessive), Huntington's disease (dominant) and Down syndrome (an extra copy of chromosome 21)."],
  ["what is a pedigree chart | how do you read a pedigree", "A **pedigree** is a family-tree chart that tracks a trait through generations. Squares are males, circles are females, shaded shapes show who has the trait, and half-shaded or dotted ones often show carriers."],
  ["what is a carrier in genetics | what does it mean to be a carrier", "A **carrier** has one copy of a recessive allele for a condition but doesn't show it, because the dominant allele masks it. Carriers can still pass the allele to their children."],
  ["what are inherited vs acquired traits | can you inherit learned traits", "**Inherited traits** come from your genes, like eye color or blood type. **Acquired traits** are developed during your life, like a scar, muscles from exercise or speaking a language, and they aren't passed on through genes."],
  ["what is a trait | examples of traits", "A **trait** is a characteristic of an organism, like eye color, height, leaf shape or fur pattern. Many traits are influenced by both genes and environment."],
  ["what are mendel's laws | law of segregation | law of independent assortment", "Mendel's **law of segregation** says each parent passes on only one of its two alleles for a gene. His **law of independent assortment** says alleles for different genes are passed on independently of each other (if the genes are on different chromosomes)."],
  ["why did mendel use pea plants | what did mendel's pea experiments show", "Gregor Mendel used **pea plants** because they grow fast, have easy-to-see traits (tall or short, purple or white flowers) and he could control which plants pollinated each other. His careful counts revealed the basic rules of heredity."],
  ["what is gene expression | how are genes turned on and off", "**Gene expression** is when a gene's instructions are actually used to make a protein. Cells switch genes on and off depending on their type and needs, which is why a skin cell and a nerve cell behave differently despite having the same DNA."],
  ["what is genetic engineering | what is a gmo", "**Genetic engineering** means directly changing an organism's DNA, often by adding a gene from another species. A **GMO** (genetically modified organism) is the result, like crops engineered to resist pests or insulin-making bacteria."],
  ["what is crispr | how does crispr work", "**CRISPR** is a gene-editing tool, adapted from a bacterial defense system, that can cut DNA at a precise spot. Scientists use it to delete, fix or insert genes, and it's being studied to treat genetic diseases."],
  ["what is cloning | how was dolly the sheep cloned", "**Cloning** makes a genetically identical copy of an organism. **Dolly the sheep** (born 1996) was the first mammal cloned from an adult cell: the nucleus of a body cell was put into an egg cell whose nucleus had been removed."],
  ["are identical twins clones | how do identical twins form", "In a way, yes! **Identical twins** form when one fertilized egg splits into two embryos, so they share nearly the same DNA. **Fraternal twins** come from two separate eggs and are as similar as regular siblings."],
  ["what is a karyotype | how do scientists look at chromosomes", "A **karyotype** is a picture of all a cell's chromosomes arranged in pairs by size. Doctors use it to check for missing or extra chromosomes."],
  ["what is a codon | what is the genetic code", "A **codon** is a group of three DNA or RNA bases that codes for one amino acid. The **genetic code** is the set of rules linking the 64 possible codons to 20 amino acids and stop signals, and it's almost the same in all living things."],
  ["what is adaptation in biology | examples of adaptations", "An **adaptation** is a trait that helps an organism survive and reproduce in its environment. Examples include a camel's hump storing fat, a cactus's spines, a polar bear's thick fur and a chameleon's color change."],
  ["what is variation in a species | why are individuals different", "**Variation** means individuals of the same species differ, like different heights or fur colors. It comes from mutations, sexual reproduction mixing genes, and environment, and it's what natural selection acts on."],
  ["what are the steps of natural selection | how does natural selection work step by step", "Natural selection works like this: individuals **vary**, more are born than can survive, those with helpful traits **survive and reproduce** more, and they **pass those traits on**. Over many generations, helpful traits become more common."],
  ["what is an example of natural selection | peppered moth example", "A classic example is the **peppered moth** in England. When factory soot darkened tree trunks, dark moths were better hidden from birds and became common; when the air was cleaned up, light moths became common again."],
  ["what is evidence for evolution | how do we know evolution happened", "Evidence for evolution includes **fossils** showing change over time, **similar bone structures** in different animals, **DNA similarities** between species, embryo development, and evolution we can watch happen, like bacteria becoming resistant to antibiotics."],
  ["what are homologous structures | homologous vs analogous structures", "**Homologous structures** have the same basic bones but different uses, like a human arm, bat wing and whale flipper, showing a common ancestor. **Analogous structures** do the same job but evolved separately, like bird wings and insect wings."],
  ["what are vestigial structures | examples of vestigial organs", "**Vestigial structures** are body parts that have lost most of their original function through evolution, like the tiny hip bones in whales, wings on flightless birds, or the human tailbone."],
  ["what is antibiotic resistance | how do bacteria become resistant", "When antibiotics are used, a few bacteria may have mutations that let them survive. They multiply and pass on the resistance, so over time the drug stops working. That's natural selection in action, and why you should finish prescribed antibiotics and not use them for viruses."],
  ["what is speciation | how do new species form", "**Speciation** is when one species splits into two. It often happens when groups get separated (for example by a river or mountain), then change differently over many generations until they can no longer interbreed."],
  ["what is artificial selection | what is selective breeding", "**Artificial selection** (selective breeding) is when humans choose which plants or animals breed to get desired traits. All dog breeds came from wolves this way, and broccoli, cabbage and kale were all bred from one wild mustard plant."],
  ["what is common ancestry | do all living things share an ancestor", "Yes, scientists think all life on Earth shares a **common ancestor** from billions of years ago. Evidence includes the fact that all living things use DNA and nearly the same genetic code."],
  ["what did darwin see on the galapagos | darwin's finches", "On the **Galapagos Islands**, Darwin noticed finches on different islands had different beak shapes suited to their foods, like thick beaks for cracking seeds and thin ones for catching insects. This helped him develop the idea of natural selection."],
  ["what is fitness in evolution | what does survival of the fittest really mean", "In evolution, **fitness** means how well an organism survives and reproduces in its environment, not how strong or athletic it is. A \"fit\" organism leaves more offspring."],
  ["what is camouflage | what is mimicry in animals", "**Camouflage** helps an animal blend into its surroundings, like a stick insect looking like a twig. **Mimicry** is when one species looks like another, like the harmless viceroy butterfly resembling the bad-tasting monarch."],
  ["what is coevolution | example of coevolution", "**Coevolution** is when two species influence each other's evolution. For example, some flowers evolved long tubes and their pollinators (like certain moths) evolved long tongues to reach the nectar."],
  ["what is classification in biology | what is taxonomy | why do we classify living things", "**Taxonomy** is the science of naming and grouping living things based on shared features and evolutionary relationships. It helps scientists organize millions of species and communicate clearly."],
  ["who invented scientific names | who came up with naming species | father of taxonomy", "**Carl Linnaeus** was an 18th-century Swedish scientist known as the father of taxonomy. He created the system of giving each species a two-part Latin name and grouping organisms into ranks."],
  ["what is binomial nomenclature | how do scientific names work", "**Binomial nomenclature** gives every species a two-part name: the **genus** (capitalized) and the **species** (lowercase), written in italics, like *Homo sapiens* for humans or *Felis catus* for house cats."],
  ["what are the levels of classification | kingdom phylum class order family genus species", "The main ranks from broadest to most specific are **domain, kingdom, phylum, class, order, family, genus, species**. A popular memory trick is \"**D**ear **K**ing **P**hilip **C**ame **O**ver **F**or **G**ood **S**oup.\""],
  ["what are the six kingdoms | list the kingdoms of life", "The six kingdoms are **Animalia**, **Plantae**, **Fungi**, **Protista**, **Eubacteria** and **Archaebacteria**. Some textbooks use five kingdoms, grouping both bacteria into Monera."],
  ["what are the three domains of life | archaea bacteria eukarya", "The three domains are **Bacteria**, **Archaea** (single-celled prokaryotes often found in extreme places) and **Eukarya** (everything with a nucleus: animals, plants, fungi and protists)."],
  ["what is the scientific name for humans | what does homo sapiens mean", "Humans are *Homo sapiens*, which is Latin for \"wise human.\" We're in the genus *Homo*, family Hominidae, order Primates, class Mammalia."],
  ["what is the scientific name for a dog | what is the scientific name of a cat", "The domestic dog is *Canis familiaris* (often listed as *Canis lupus familiaris*, a subspecies of the wolf), and the house cat is *Felis catus*."],
  ["what are archaea | what are archaebacteria", "**Archaea** are single-celled prokaryotes that look like bacteria but are chemically quite different. Many live in extreme places like hot springs, salty lakes and deep-sea vents, but they're also found in soil, oceans and even your gut."],
  ["what are protists | examples of protists", "**Protists** are mostly single-celled eukaryotes that aren't animals, plants or fungi. Examples include amoebas, paramecia, algae and the euglena. Some cause disease, like the parasite that causes malaria."],
  ["what is an amoeba | how does an amoeba move", "An **amoeba** is a single-celled protist that changes shape as it moves. It pushes out finger-like extensions called **pseudopods** (\"false feet\") to crawl and to surround and swallow food."],
  ["what is a paramecium | how does a paramecium move", "A **paramecium** is a slipper-shaped single-celled protist covered in tiny hairs called **cilia**, which beat together to make it swim through pond water."],
  ["what is a dichotomous key | how do you use a dichotomous key", "A **dichotomous key** is a tool for identifying living things by answering a series of two-choice questions, like \"Does it have feathers? Yes or no.\" Each answer leads to the next step until you reach a name."],
  ["what are vertebrates and invertebrates | vertebrate vs invertebrate", "**Vertebrates** have a backbone: fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals. **Invertebrates** don't, and they make up about 97% of animal species, including insects, spiders, worms, jellyfish and snails."],
  ["what are the five classes of vertebrates | groups of vertebrates", "The five main vertebrate groups are **fish, amphibians, reptiles, birds and mammals**. Fish actually include several separate classes, like bony fish and cartilaginous fish (sharks)."],
  ["what is an arthropod | examples of arthropods", "**Arthropods** are invertebrates with an exoskeleton, a segmented body and jointed legs. They include insects, spiders, crabs, lobsters, centipedes and millipedes, and are the largest animal group."],
  ["difference between insects and spiders | is a spider an insect", "A spider is **not** an insect; it's an arachnid. Insects have **6 legs**, 3 body parts and usually antennae and wings. Spiders have **8 legs**, 2 body parts, no antennae and no wings."],
  ["what is a biotic factor | biotic vs abiotic factors", "**Biotic factors** are the living parts of an ecosystem, like plants, animals and bacteria. **Abiotic factors** are non-living, like sunlight, water, temperature, soil and air."],
  ["what is a habitat vs a niche | what is an ecological niche", "A **habitat** is where an organism lives, like a pond. Its **niche** is its role there: what it eats, what eats it, when it's active and how it interacts with everything else."],
  ["what is a population vs community in ecology | ecology levels of organization", "In ecology, a **population** is all the members of one species in an area. A **community** is all the populations of different species living together. Add the non-living environment and you have an **ecosystem**, and all ecosystems together form the **biosphere**."],
  ["what are producers consumers and decomposers | roles in a food chain", "**Producers** (plants, algae) make their own food from sunlight. **Consumers** eat other organisms. **Decomposers** (bacteria, fungi) break down dead things and return nutrients to the soil."],
  ["what is a herbivore carnivore and omnivore | difference between herbivore and carnivore", "**Herbivores** eat only plants (like cows and deer), **carnivores** eat other animals (like lions and sharks), and **omnivores** eat both (like bears, pigs and most humans)."],
  ["what are primary and secondary consumers | trophic levels", "**Trophic levels** are the steps in a food chain. Producers are level 1, **primary consumers** (herbivores) eat them, **secondary consumers** eat herbivores, and **tertiary consumers** eat secondary consumers."],
  ["what is an energy pyramid | the 10 percent rule", "An **energy pyramid** shows how energy decreases up a food chain. Only about **10%** of the energy at one level is passed to the next; the rest is used for living or lost as heat. That's why there are fewer top predators."],
  ["what is a scavenger | scavenger vs decomposer", "A **scavenger** is an animal that eats dead animals it didn't kill, like vultures and hyenas. **Decomposers** like fungi and bacteria break down the remains even further into nutrients."],
  ["what is symbiosis | types of symbiosis", "**Symbiosis** is a close relationship between two species. The three types are **mutualism** (both benefit), **commensalism** (one benefits, the other isn't affected) and **parasitism** (one benefits, the other is harmed)."],
  ["what is mutualism | example of mutualism", "**Mutualism** is a relationship where both species benefit. Bees get nectar while pollinating flowers, and clownfish get protection in sea anemones while helping keep them clean."],
  ["what is commensalism | example of commensalism", "**Commensalism** is when one species benefits and the other isn't helped or harmed. For example, barnacles riding on a whale get a free trip to food, and birds nesting in trees get shelter."],
  ["what is parasitism | what is a parasite", "A **parasite** lives on or in a host and benefits at the host's expense. Examples include ticks, fleas, tapeworms and mistletoe, which steals water and nutrients from trees."],
  ["what is predation | predator and prey relationship", "**Predation** is when one animal (the predator) hunts and eats another (the prey). Predator and prey populations often rise and fall in cycles, like lynx and snowshoe hares."],
  ["what is competition in ecology | why do organisms compete", "**Competition** happens when organisms need the same limited resources, like food, water, light or territory. It can be between members of the same species or between different species."],
  ["what is carrying capacity | what limits population growth", "**Carrying capacity** is the largest population an environment can support long-term. It's set by **limiting factors** like food, water, space, predators and disease."],
  ["what is ecological succession | primary vs secondary succession", "**Succession** is the gradual change of a community over time. **Primary succession** starts on bare rock with no soil (after a lava flow), beginning with lichens; **secondary succession** happens where soil remains, like after a forest fire."],
  ["what is a pioneer species | examples of pioneer species", "**Pioneer species** are the first organisms to grow on bare land, like **lichens** and mosses. They break down rock and add organic matter, slowly making soil so other plants can move in."],
  ["what is the nitrogen cycle | how do plants get nitrogen", "Plants can't use nitrogen gas from the air directly. In the **nitrogen cycle**, bacteria in soil and root nodules \"fix\" nitrogen into compounds like ammonia and nitrates that plants absorb; decomposers and other bacteria later return it to the air."],
  ["what is bioaccumulation | what is biomagnification", "**Biomagnification** is when toxic substances, like mercury or the pesticide DDT, become more concentrated at each step up a food chain. Top predators like eagles and large fish end up with the highest levels."],
  ["what is a microorganism example | what are the main types of microorganisms", "The main types of microorganisms are **bacteria**, **archaea**, **fungi** (like yeast and molds), **protists** (like amoebas and algae) and **viruses**, though viruses aren't usually considered truly alive."],
  ["what shapes are bacteria | bacteria shapes cocci bacilli spirilla", "Bacteria come in three main shapes: **cocci** (round, like balls), **bacilli** (rods) and **spirilla** (spirals). Scientists use shape as one way to identify them."],
  ["how do bacteria reproduce | what is binary fission", "Bacteria reproduce by **binary fission**: the cell copies its DNA, grows, and splits into two identical cells. Under good conditions some bacteria can divide every 20 minutes."],
  ["what are good bacteria | helpful bacteria examples", "Helpful bacteria live in your gut and help digest food and make vitamins. Others turn milk into yogurt and cheese, recycle nutrients in soil and fix nitrogen for plants."],
  ["what is yeast | is yeast alive | how does yeast make bread rise", "**Yeast** is a living single-celled fungus. In bread dough it eats sugars and releases **carbon dioxide** gas through fermentation, and the bubbles make the bread rise."],
  ["what is mold | how does mold grow", "**Mold** is a type of fungus that grows as fuzzy threads called hyphae. It spreads by tiny spores in the air and grows on damp food, walls and fabric. Some molds are useful, like the one that makes penicillin."],
  ["what is fermentation | how does fermentation work", "**Fermentation** is a process where microbes like yeast or bacteria break down sugars without oxygen. Yeast makes carbon dioxide and alcohol, and some bacteria make lactic acid, which is how yogurt, sauerkraut and sourdough are made."],
  ["what is a pathogen | what causes infectious diseases", "A **pathogen** is a germ that causes disease. The main types are bacteria (strep throat), viruses (flu, colds), fungi (athlete's foot) and parasites like protists (malaria)."],
  ["how big are bacteria compared to viruses | which is smaller a virus or bacteria", "Viruses are much smaller. A typical bacterium is about 1 to 2 micrometers long, while most viruses are 20 to 300 nanometers, often 10 to 100 times smaller, so you need an electron microscope to see them."],
  ["how do viruses reproduce | what is the lytic cycle", "A virus can't reproduce by itself. It injects its genetic material into a host cell and hijacks the cell's machinery to make new viruses; in the **lytic cycle**, the cell bursts open and releases them."],
  ["what are lichens | is a lichen a plant", "A **lichen** isn't a plant. It's a partnership between a **fungus** and an **alga** or cyanobacterium: the alga makes food by photosynthesis, and the fungus provides shelter and moisture. Lichens can grow on bare rock."],
  ["what are tardigrades | what is a water bear", "**Tardigrades**, or water bears, are tiny eight-legged animals about half a millimeter long. They can survive extreme cold, heat, dryness and even the vacuum of space by drying out into a dormant state."],
  ["what is a food web vs food chain difference", "A **food chain** shows one path of energy, like grass to rabbit to fox. A **food web** shows many connected food chains in an ecosystem, which is more realistic because most animals eat and are eaten by several species."],
  ["what is a decomposer example | examples of decomposers", "Common decomposers include **bacteria**, **fungi** like mushrooms and molds, and detritivores like **earthworms**, millipedes and woodlice that help break down dead plants and animals."],
  ["what is homeostasis | examples of homeostasis", "**Homeostasis** is how living things keep their internal conditions stable. For example, you sweat to cool down, shiver to warm up, and your body controls your blood sugar and water levels."],
  ["what are the characteristics of living things | how do you know if something is alive", "Living things are made of **cells**, use **energy**, **grow and develop**, **reproduce**, **respond** to their environment, keep **homeostasis**, and their species **adapt** over generations."],
  ["what is asexual reproduction | sexual vs asexual reproduction", "**Asexual reproduction** needs one parent and makes genetically identical offspring, like bacteria splitting or a strawberry plant sending out runners. **Sexual reproduction** combines genes from two parents, making offspring that are different."],
  ["what is regeneration | which animals can regrow body parts", "**Regeneration** is regrowing lost body parts. Starfish can regrow arms, salamanders like the axolotl can regrow legs, and flatworms called planarians can regrow a whole body from a small piece."],
  ["what is a hybrid animal | what is a mule", "A **hybrid** is the offspring of two different species. A **mule** comes from a male donkey and a female horse; mules are strong but almost always sterile, so they can't have babies."],
  ["what are the parts of a plant | main parts of a plant and their functions", "The main parts of a plant are the **roots** (anchor it and absorb water and minerals), the **stem** (supports it and carries water and food), the **leaves** (make food by photosynthesis), and **flowers**, **fruits** and **seeds** for reproduction."],
  ["what do roots do | function of roots | why do plants have roots", "**Roots** hold the plant in the ground, absorb water and minerals from the soil, and often store food. Tiny **root hairs** greatly increase the surface area for taking in water."],
  ["taproot vs fibrous root | what is a taproot", "A **taproot** is one thick main root growing straight down, like a carrot or dandelion. **Fibrous roots** are many thin roots spreading out near the surface, like grass."],
  ["what does the stem do | function of a plant stem", "The **stem** holds the plant up toward the light and acts as a transport highway, carrying water up from the roots and sugars from the leaves to the rest of the plant."],
  ["what is xylem and phloem | difference between xylem and phloem", "**Xylem** tubes carry water and minerals **up** from the roots to the leaves. **Phloem** tubes carry sugars made in the leaves to all parts of the plant, both up and down."],
  ["what do leaves do | function of leaves | parts of a leaf", "**Leaves** are the plant's food factories, where photosynthesis happens. A leaf has a flat **blade** to catch sunlight, **veins** to carry water and food, and a stalk called a **petiole** attaching it to the stem."],
  ["what are stomata | what do stomata do | what are guard cells", "**Stomata** are tiny pores, mostly on the underside of leaves, that let carbon dioxide in and oxygen and water vapor out. Each is surrounded by two **guard cells** that open and close it."],
  ["how does water move up a plant | how do plants pull water up | transpiration pull", "**Transpiration** is the loss of water vapor from leaves through the stomata. As water evaporates, it pulls more water up through the xylem from the roots, like sucking through a straw."],
  ["what does a plant need to survive | what are the needs of a plant", "Plants need **light**, **water**, **carbon dioxide** from the air, **nutrients** (minerals) from the soil, the right **temperature** and space to grow. Most also need oxygen for their roots."],
  ["where does a plant's mass come from | do plants get their mass from soil", "Surprisingly, most of a plant's mass comes from the **air**! Plants build sugars and cellulose from **carbon dioxide** captured during photosynthesis, plus water. Soil provides only small amounts of minerals."],
  ["what is the difference between photosynthesis and respiration | photosynthesis vs respiration", "**Photosynthesis** uses light, CO2 and water to make glucose and oxygen, and it happens only in cells with chloroplasts. **Respiration** breaks down glucose with oxygen to release energy, giving off CO2 and water, and it happens in all living cells day and night."],
  ["do plants breathe | do plants respire at night", "Yes! Plants do **cellular respiration** all the time, day and night, taking in oxygen and releasing carbon dioxide. During the day photosynthesis is usually faster, so overall they give off more oxygen than they use."],
  ["what are the products of photosynthesis | what do plants make in photosynthesis", "Photosynthesis produces **glucose** (sugar), which the plant uses for energy and to build things like cellulose and starch, and **oxygen**, which is released into the air."],
  ["where does photosynthesis happen | which part of the plant does photosynthesis", "Photosynthesis happens mainly in the **leaves**, inside **chloroplasts** in the palisade cells just under the leaf's surface. Green stems can do some photosynthesis too."],
  ["what affects the rate of photosynthesis | limiting factors photosynthesis", "The rate of photosynthesis depends on **light intensity**, **carbon dioxide** level and **temperature**. Whichever is in shortest supply is the limiting factor; too much heat can also damage the enzymes involved."],
  ["what are the light dependent reactions | what is the calvin cycle", "Photosynthesis has two stages. The **light-dependent reactions** capture light energy, split water and release oxygen. The **Calvin cycle** (light-independent reactions) then uses that energy to turn CO2 into sugar."],
  ["how do plants store food | what is starch in plants", "Plants turn extra glucose into **starch** for storage, often in roots, tubers, seeds and stems. Potatoes, rice and wheat are all full of stored starch, which is why they're good food for us."],
  ["what are the parts of a flower | flower parts and functions", "A flower has **petals** to attract pollinators, **sepals** that protect the bud, the male **stamen** (anther and filament) that makes pollen, and the female **pistil** (stigma, style and ovary) that holds the ovules."],
  ["what is the stamen | what is the anther", "The **stamen** is the male part of a flower. It has a thin stalk called the **filament** topped by the **anther**, which makes pollen."],
  ["what is the pistil | what is the stigma style and ovary | what is a carpel", "The **pistil** (or carpel) is the female part of a flower. The sticky **stigma** catches pollen, the **style** is a tube leading down, and the **ovary** holds ovules that become seeds after fertilization."],
  ["self pollination vs cross pollination | what is cross pollination", "In **self-pollination**, pollen fertilizes the same flower or another flower on the same plant. In **cross-pollination**, pollen comes from a different plant of the same species, which mixes genes and creates more variety."],
  ["what are pollinators | which animals pollinate plants", "**Pollinators** carry pollen between flowers. Bees are the most important, but butterflies, moths, beetles, flies, hummingbirds and bats are pollinators too, and some plants rely on the wind."],
  ["what happens if bees disappear | why are bees important for food crops", "Bees pollinate many of the crops we eat, like apples, almonds, berries and squash, and many wild plants. Without them, lots of foods would become scarce and ecosystems would suffer."],
  ["how does wind pollination work | which plants are wind pollinated", "Wind-pollinated plants, like grasses, corn, oaks and pine trees, release huge amounts of light pollen into the air. Their flowers are usually small and plain because they don't need to attract animals. This pollen is what triggers many hay fever allergies."],
  ["what happens after pollination | how do flowers become fruit", "After pollination, a pollen tube grows down to the ovule and **fertilization** happens. The ovules develop into **seeds**, and the ovary around them swells to become the **fruit**, while the petals usually fall off."],
  ["what is a fruit in botany | is a tomato a fruit", "In botany, a **fruit** is the ripened ovary of a flower that contains seeds. So yes, **tomatoes**, cucumbers, peppers, pumpkins and even peas in the pod are botanically fruits, even though we cook them as vegetables."],
  ["what are the parts of a seed | what is inside a seed", "A seed has three main parts: the **seed coat** that protects it, the **embryo** (a tiny baby plant), and stored food, often in the **cotyledons** (seed leaves) or endosperm."],
  ["how are seeds dispersed | ways seeds spread", "Seeds are spread by **wind** (dandelion parachutes, maple \"helicopters\"), **water** (coconuts), **animals** (eaten in fruit or hooked onto fur like burrs) and even by **bursting** pods that fling them out."],
  ["why do plants spread their seeds | why is seed dispersal important", "Spreading seeds away from the parent plant means the new seedlings don't have to compete with it for light, water and nutrients. It also helps the species colonize new areas."],
  ["what is germination | how does a seed germinate", "**Germination** is when a seed starts to grow. It soaks up water, swells, the seed coat splits, and first a root (radicle) grows down, then a shoot grows up toward the light."],
  ["what do seeds need to germinate | conditions for germination", "Most seeds need **water**, **oxygen** and a suitable **temperature** to germinate. Most don't need light until the seedling comes up, though a few tiny seeds like lettuce do need light to sprout."],
  ["what is seed dormancy | how long can seeds survive", "**Dormancy** is when a seed stays alive but inactive until conditions are right. Some seeds can wait for years; a date palm was successfully grown from a seed about 2,000 years old!"],
  ["what is a monocot vs dicot | difference between monocots and dicots", "**Monocots** (like grass, corn and lilies) have one seed leaf, parallel leaf veins and flower parts in threes. **Dicots** (like beans, roses and oaks) have two seed leaves, net-like veins and flower parts in fours or fives."],
  ["what is a cotyledon | what are seed leaves", "**Cotyledons** are the first leaves that come from a seed. They often store food for the seedling and look different from the plant's later \"true\" leaves."],
  ["what is a tropism | types of tropisms", "A **tropism** is a plant growing toward or away from a stimulus. The main ones are **phototropism** (light), **gravitropism** or geotropism (gravity), **hydrotropism** (water) and **thigmotropism** (touch)."],
  ["what is phototropism | why do plants grow toward light", "**Phototropism** is growth toward light. A hormone called **auxin** collects on the shaded side of a stem and makes those cells grow longer, bending the stem toward the light."],
  ["what is gravitropism | what is geotropism | how do roots know which way is down", "**Gravitropism** (geotropism) is growth in response to gravity. Roots grow **down** with gravity and shoots grow **up** against it, even if a seed is planted upside down."],
  ["what is thigmotropism | how do vines climb | why do tendrils curl", "**Thigmotropism** is growth in response to touch. When a vine's tendril touches a pole or fence, the cells on the other side grow faster, so it curls around and climbs."],
  ["what are plant hormones | what is auxin", "Plant hormones control growth. **Auxin** makes cells lengthen and causes tropisms, **gibberellins** help stems grow and seeds germinate, **ethylene** ripens fruit, and **cytokinins** encourage cell division."],
  ["why does a banana ripen other fruit | ripening fruit in a paper bag", "Ripening fruits like bananas give off **ethylene** gas, a plant hormone that speeds up ripening. Putting an unripe fruit in a paper bag with a banana traps the ethylene and makes it ripen faster."],
  ["what is a nastic movement | why does the sensitive plant close its leaves | how does a venus flytrap close", "Some plants move quickly regardless of direction, which is called a **nastic movement**. The sensitive plant (*Mimosa pudica*) folds its leaves when touched, and a Venus flytrap snaps shut when trigger hairs inside are touched twice."],
  ["how do carnivorous plants digest insects | why do carnivorous plants eat bugs", "**Carnivorous plants**, like Venus flytraps, pitcher plants and sundews, trap and digest insects. They still photosynthesize, but they grow in poor soils and get extra nitrogen from their prey."],
  ["how do trees grow taller | do trees grow from the top", "Trees grow taller only at the **tips** of their branches and trunk, from growing zones called meristems. A nail hammered into a trunk stays at the same height as the tree grows."],
  ["what are tree rings | what do tree rings tell us | why do trees have growth rings", "Each year a tree adds a new layer of wood, making a **growth ring**: light wood in spring, dark wood in late summer. Counting the rings on a stump tells you its age, and wider rings mean better growing years."],
  ["what are the parts of a tree trunk | what is bark for", "A tree trunk has **outer bark** for protection, **inner bark** (phloem) carrying sugars, a thin **cambium** layer that makes new wood and bark, **sapwood** carrying water, and dead central **heartwood** for strength."],
  ["deciduous vs evergreen trees | what is a deciduous tree", "**Deciduous** trees, like oaks and maples, lose all their leaves in fall to save water and energy in winter. **Evergreen** trees, like pines and spruces, keep their leaves or needles all year and replace them gradually."],
  ["what is a conifer | do pine trees have flowers", "A **conifer** is a tree that makes seeds in **cones** instead of flowers, like pines, spruces, firs and redwoods. They're gymnosperms, and most have needle-like or scale-like leaves."],
  ["gymnosperms vs angiosperms | what is an angiosperm", "**Angiosperms** are flowering plants whose seeds are enclosed in fruits, like apple trees and roses; they're the largest plant group. **Gymnosperms** have \"naked\" seeds, usually on cones, like pines and ginkgos."],
  ["what are vascular and nonvascular plants | what is a nonvascular plant", "**Vascular plants** have xylem and phloem tubes to move water and food, letting them grow tall, like ferns, trees and flowers. **Nonvascular plants**, like mosses, lack these tubes, so they stay small and live in damp places."],
  ["what are mosses | how do mosses reproduce", "**Mosses** are small nonvascular plants that grow in soft carpets in damp, shady places. They have no true roots, flowers or seeds and reproduce with **spores** released from little capsules on stalks."],
  ["what are ferns | how do ferns reproduce", "**Ferns** are vascular plants with feathery leaves called fronds. They don't make flowers or seeds; instead they reproduce with **spores**, which you can often see as brown dots on the undersides of the fronds."],
  ["what are spores | spores vs seeds", "**Spores** are tiny single cells that can grow into a new organism; ferns, mosses and fungi use them. **Seeds** are more complex, holding a baby plant embryo plus stored food inside a protective coat."],
  ["how do mushrooms reproduce | what part of a mushroom is the fungus", "The mushroom you see is just the fruiting body of a fungus. It releases millions of **spores** from its gills. The main part of the fungus is the **mycelium**, a network of thin threads called hyphae hidden in soil or wood."],
  ["how do fungi get food | are fungi more like plants or animals", "Fungi can't photosynthesize; they absorb nutrients from dead or living matter around them by releasing digestive enzymes. Genetically, fungi are actually more closely related to **animals** than to plants."],
  ["what is the largest living organism | what is the humongous fungus", "One of the largest organisms on Earth is a **honey fungus** in Oregon's Blue Mountains, whose underground mycelium spreads across nearly 10 square kilometers. The Pando aspen colony in Utah is another giant contender."],
  ["what is mycorrhiza | how do fungi help trees", "**Mycorrhizae** are partnerships between fungi and plant roots. The fungal threads help roots absorb water and minerals, and the plant gives the fungus sugars. Most land plants depend on this teamwork."],
  ["what are algae | are algae plants", "**Algae** are simple, mostly water-living organisms that photosynthesize, ranging from single cells to giant seaweeds like kelp. They're usually classed as protists, not true plants, and they produce a large share of Earth's oxygen."],
  ["what is vegetative propagation | how do plants reproduce asexually", "**Vegetative propagation** is when a new plant grows from part of the parent, like a potato sprouting from its \"eyes,\" strawberry runners, spider plant babies, or a cutting placed in water or soil."],
  ["how do you grow a plant from a cutting | how to propagate a plant", "Cut a healthy stem about 10 to 15 cm long just below a leaf node, remove the lower leaves and place it in water or moist potting mix. Keep it warm and in bright, indirect light, and roots usually form within a few weeks."],
  ["how do i grow a bean plant | how to grow beans from seed", "Soak bean seeds overnight, then plant them about 2 to 3 cm deep in moist soil, or wrap them in a damp paper towel in a clear bag to watch them sprout. Keep them warm and watered, and move them to a sunny spot once leaves appear."],
  ["how do i grow tomatoes | tips for growing tomatoes", "Tomatoes need **full sun** (6 to 8 hours a day), warm weather and rich, well-drained soil. Plant seedlings deeply, water at the base regularly, and support them with stakes or cages as they grow."],
  ["how do you grow sunflowers | how to plant sunflower seeds", "Plant sunflower seeds about 2 to 3 cm deep in a **sunny** spot after the last frost, spacing them well apart. Water regularly, and tall varieties may need a stake. They can bloom in about 2 to 3 months."],
  ["how do i grow an avocado from a seed | how to grow avocado pit", "Wash the pit, poke three toothpicks into its sides and rest it, pointy end up, over a glass of water so the bottom half is submerged. Keep it warm and change the water often; a root and shoot should appear within 2 to 8 weeks, then plant it in soil."],
  ["how do you grow potatoes | how to plant potatoes", "Cut seed potatoes into chunks with at least one or two \"eyes\" each, let them dry a day, then plant them about 10 cm deep in loose soil in a sunny spot. As the plants grow, pile more soil around the stems; harvest after the plants flower and the leaves die back."],
  ["how do i grow a cactus | how often to water a cactus", "Cacti need **lots of light**, a gritty, fast-draining soil and a pot with drainage holes. Water deeply only when the soil is completely dry, which may be every few weeks, and much less in winter."],
  ["why are my plant's leaves turning yellow | yellow leaves on plant", "Yellow leaves are most often caused by **overwatering**, which suffocates roots. Other causes include too little light, lack of nutrients (especially nitrogen), underwatering or the natural shedding of old leaves."],
  ["why is my plant wilting | what causes wilting", "A plant wilts when its cells lose water and the vacuoles shrink, so the stems and leaves go limp. It's usually from **too little water** or heat, but soggy, rotting roots from overwatering can cause it too."],
  ["what is fertilizer | what do npk numbers mean", "**Fertilizer** adds nutrients to soil. The three numbers (N-P-K) show the amount of **nitrogen** (leafy growth), **phosphorus** (roots and flowers) and **potassium** (overall health and disease resistance)."],
  ["what is hydroponics | can plants grow without soil", "Yes! **Hydroponics** grows plants with their roots in water containing dissolved nutrients instead of soil. Plants only need soil for support and minerals, so hydroponics supplies those another way."],
  ["what are annuals perennials and biennials | annual vs perennial plants", "**Annuals** complete their life cycle in one year, like marigolds. **Biennials** take two years, like carrots and foxgloves. **Perennials** live for many years, like trees, tulips and most shrubs."],
  ["what is a bulb | what is a tuber | bulb vs tuber", "A **bulb** is an underground bud surrounded by fleshy leaves that store food, like an onion or tulip. A **tuber** is a swollen underground stem or root storing food, like a potato or sweet potato."],
  ["how do plants defend themselves | plant defenses", "Plants defend themselves with **thorns** and spines, stinging hairs (nettles), tough or waxy leaves, and **chemicals** that taste bad or are toxic. Some even release scents that attract predators of the insects eating them."],
  ["desert plant adaptations | how do cacti store water", "Cacti store water in thick, fleshy stems, have **spines** instead of leaves to reduce water loss and deter animals, a waxy coating, and wide shallow roots to soak up rain fast. Many open their stomata only at night."],
  ["what is the rafflesia | rafflesia flower facts", "The **Rafflesia arnoldii** of Southeast Asia has the largest single flower, up to about 1 meter across. It's a parasite with no leaves and smells like rotting meat to attract flies."],
  ["what is chlorophyll a and b | why do leaves have other pigments", "Leaves have several pigments: **chlorophyll a** and **b** absorb red and blue light, and **carotenoids** (yellow and orange) help capture more light and protect the leaf. You see the carotenoids in fall when the chlorophyll breaks down."],
  ["do plants feel pain | do plants have feelings", "Plants don't have brains or nerves, so they can't feel pain like we do. They can still sense and respond to damage, light, touch and gravity using chemical and electrical signals."],
  ["what is botany | what is a botanist", "**Botany** is the scientific study of plants. A **botanist** studies how plants grow, reproduce, are classified, and interact with their environment."],
  ["what is matter | define matter", "**Matter** is anything that has mass and takes up space. Everything you can touch, plus air and water, is matter, and it's all made of atoms."],
  ["what is the nucleus of an atom | what is in the nucleus of an atom", "The **nucleus** is the tiny, dense center of an atom, made of **protons** and **neutrons**. It holds almost all of the atom's mass, even though it's about 100,000 times smaller than the whole atom."],
  ["what is the charge of a proton neutron and electron | which particles are positive and negative", "**Protons** have a positive (+1) charge, **electrons** have a negative (-1) charge, and **neutrons** have no charge. In a neutral atom, the number of protons equals the number of electrons."],
  ["what is the mass of an electron | which subatomic particle is the lightest", "The **electron** is by far the lightest, about 1/1836 the mass of a proton. Protons and neutrons have almost the same mass, about 1 atomic mass unit each."],
  ["what is atomic number | what does the atomic number tell you", "The **atomic number** is the number of protons in an atom's nucleus. It defines the element: every carbon atom has 6 protons, every oxygen atom has 8."],
  ["what is mass number | how do you find the number of neutrons", "The **mass number** is the total number of protons plus neutrons. To find the neutrons, subtract the atomic number from the mass number; for example, carbon-12 has 12 - 6 = **6 neutrons**."],
  ["what is atomic mass | why is atomic mass a decimal", "**Atomic mass** is the average mass of an element's atoms, measured in atomic mass units. It's usually a decimal because it averages all the element's natural isotopes, weighted by how common each is."],
  ["what are electron shells | how many electrons in each shell | what are energy levels", "Electrons orbit the nucleus in **shells** (energy levels). In the simple school model, the first shell holds up to **2** electrons, the second up to **8**, and the third holds 8 for the first 20 elements."],
  ["what are valence electrons | why do valence electrons matter", "**Valence electrons** are the electrons in an atom's outermost shell. They decide how the atom bonds and reacts; atoms tend to gain, lose or share them to get a full outer shell."],
  ["what is the octet rule", "The **octet rule** says atoms tend to gain, lose or share electrons until they have **8** electrons in their outer shell, which is a stable arrangement like the noble gases."],
  ["what is a bohr model | how do you draw a bohr model", "A **Bohr model** shows an atom with the nucleus in the center and electrons in circular shells around it. To draw one, put the protons and neutrons in the middle, then fill shells with electrons: 2, then 8, then 8."],
  ["what is the electron cloud model | how do electrons actually move", "In the modern **electron cloud model**, electrons don't travel in neat orbits. Instead, they exist in fuzzy regions called **orbitals** where they are most likely to be found."],
  ["who discovered the electron | who discovered the proton | who discovered the neutron", "**J.J. Thomson** discovered the electron in 1897, **Ernest Rutherford** identified the proton (and discovered the nucleus) around 1909 to 1919, and **James Chadwick** discovered the neutron in 1932."],
  ["what was rutherford's gold foil experiment | what did the gold foil experiment show", "Rutherford fired alpha particles at thin gold foil. Most went straight through, but a few bounced back, showing that atoms are mostly **empty space** with a tiny, dense, positive **nucleus**."],
  ["what is the plum pudding model", "The **plum pudding model** was J.J. Thomson's 1904 idea of the atom: a ball of positive charge with negative electrons scattered through it like plums in a pudding. Rutherford's gold foil experiment later disproved it."],
  ["how does the periodic table work | how do you read the periodic table", "The periodic table lists elements in order of **atomic number**. Rows are called **periods**, and columns are **groups**; elements in the same group have the same number of valence electrons, so they behave similarly."],
  ["how did mendeleev make the periodic table | how did mendeleev predict new elements", "**Dmitri Mendeleev**, a Russian chemist, published the first widely used periodic table in 1869. He even left gaps for elements not yet discovered and correctly predicted their properties."],
  ["what are periods and groups on the periodic table | what is a period in chemistry", "**Periods** are the horizontal rows; all elements in a period have the same number of electron shells. **Groups** (or families) are the vertical columns, numbered 1 to 18, with similar chemical properties."],
  ["what are metals nonmetals and metalloids | where are metals on the periodic table", "**Metals** are on the left and middle of the periodic table: shiny, good conductors and bendable. **Nonmetals** are on the upper right: dull, poor conductors and brittle as solids. **Metalloids** like silicon sit along the zigzag line and have properties of both."],
  ["what are the alkali metals | group 1 elements", "The **alkali metals** are in group 1: lithium, sodium, potassium, rubidium, cesium and francium. They're soft, have one valence electron and react strongly with water, getting more reactive as you go down the group."],
  ["what are the alkaline earth metals | group 2 elements", "The **alkaline earth metals** are in group 2: beryllium, magnesium, calcium, strontium, barium and radium. They have two valence electrons and are reactive, though less so than the alkali metals."],
  ["what are the transition metals | what are groups 3 to 12", "The **transition metals** fill groups 3 to 12 in the middle of the table. They include iron, copper, gold, silver, nickel and zinc; they're usually hard, shiny, good conductors and often form colorful compounds."],
  ["what are the halogens | group 17 elements", "The **halogens** are in group 17: fluorine, chlorine, bromine, iodine and astatine. They have seven valence electrons, are very reactive nonmetals, and combine with metals to form salts (halogen means \"salt-former\")."],
  ["what are the noble gases | why are noble gases unreactive | group 18 elements", "The **noble gases** are in group 18: helium, neon, argon, krypton, xenon and radon. They have full outer electron shells, so they're very stable and rarely react with anything."],
  ["what are the lanthanides and actinides | why are two rows at the bottom of the periodic table", "The two rows at the bottom are the **lanthanides** and **actinides**. They really belong in periods 6 and 7 but are placed below to keep the table from getting too wide. Many actinides, like uranium, are radioactive."],
  ["what are rare earth elements | what are rare earths used for", "**Rare earth elements** are the 15 lanthanides plus scandium and yttrium. They're used in strong magnets, phone screens, electric motors, wind turbines and lasers. They're not actually that rare, just hard to mine in concentrated form."],
  ["what are the trends on the periodic table | does atomic size increase down a group", "Atoms get **bigger** going down a group (more shells) and **smaller** going left to right across a period (more protons pull electrons closer). Metals get more reactive going down, while halogens get more reactive going up."],
  ["what is an ion | what are cations and anions", "An **ion** is an atom or molecule with an electric charge because it has gained or lost electrons. A **cation** is positive (lost electrons, like Na+), and an **anion** is negative (gained electrons, like Cl-)."],
  ["how do you tell the charge of an ion | why does sodium form a plus one ion", "Atoms lose or gain electrons to get a full outer shell. Sodium has 1 valence electron, so it loses it to become **Na+**; chlorine has 7, so it gains 1 to become **Cl-**. Group 2 metals form 2+ ions and group 16 nonmetals form 2- ions."],
  ["what are isotopes examples | what are the isotopes of carbon | what is carbon 14", "Carbon has three natural isotopes: **carbon-12** (6 neutrons, about 99%), **carbon-13** (7 neutrons) and **carbon-14** (8 neutrons), which is radioactive and used in radiocarbon dating. They all have 6 protons."],
  ["what is radioactive decay | what is a radioactive isotope | alpha beta and gamma radiation", "A **radioactive isotope** has an unstable nucleus that breaks down over time, giving off radiation. This **radioactive decay** can release alpha particles, beta particles or gamma rays and may change the atom into a different element."],
  ["what is half life | what does half life mean", "**Half-life** is the time it takes for half of a radioactive sample to decay. Carbon-14's half-life is about **5,730 years**, while some isotopes have half-lives of seconds and others billions of years."],
  ["what is a chemical bond | why do atoms bond", "A **chemical bond** is a force holding atoms together. Atoms bond to become more stable, usually by getting full outer electron shells through transferring or sharing electrons."],
  ["what is an ionic bond | how does an ionic bond form", "An **ionic bond** forms when one atom (usually a metal) **gives** electrons to another (usually a nonmetal). The resulting positive and negative ions attract each other strongly, like in table salt, NaCl."],
  ["what is a covalent bond | how does a covalent bond form", "A **covalent bond** forms when two atoms, usually nonmetals, **share** a pair of electrons. Water (H2O), oxygen gas (O2) and methane (CH4) are held together by covalent bonds."],
  ["ionic vs covalent bonds | difference between ionic and covalent", "In **ionic** bonds electrons are transferred (metal + nonmetal), making crystals with high melting points that conduct electricity when dissolved. In **covalent** bonds electrons are shared (nonmetal + nonmetal), often forming molecules with lower melting points."],
  ["what is a metallic bond | why do metals conduct electricity", "In a **metallic bond**, metal atoms share a \"sea\" of free-moving electrons. Those free electrons let metals conduct electricity and heat easily, and they let metals bend without breaking."],
  ["what is a double bond | what is a triple bond", "A **double bond** is when two atoms share two pairs of electrons, like in O2 or carbon dioxide (O=C=O). A **triple bond** shares three pairs, like in nitrogen gas (N2), which makes it very strong."],
  ["what is a polar molecule | why is water polar", "A **polar molecule** has an uneven charge, with one end slightly positive and the other slightly negative. Water is polar because oxygen pulls the shared electrons more strongly than hydrogen does, and the molecule is bent."],
  ["what is a hydrogen bond | why does water have high surface tension", "A **hydrogen bond** is a weak attraction between a slightly positive hydrogen on one molecule and a slightly negative atom (like oxygen) on another. Hydrogen bonds between water molecules cause high surface tension and water's high boiling point."],
  ["what is a chemical formula | how do you read a chemical formula", "A **chemical formula** shows which elements are in a substance and how many atoms of each. In **C6H12O6** (glucose), there are 6 carbon, 12 hydrogen and 6 oxygen atoms; a small number after a symbol counts that atom."],
  ["are all molecules compounds | molecule vs compound", "Not all molecules are compounds. A **molecule** is two or more atoms bonded together, while a **compound** must contain at least two **different** elements. So O2 is a molecule but not a compound, and H2O is both."],
  ["what are examples of mixtures | mixture examples", "A **mixture** is two or more substances physically combined but not chemically bonded, so they keep their own properties. Examples are salad, trail mix, salt water, air and sand with iron filings."],
  ["homogeneous vs heterogeneous mixture | what is a homogeneous mixture", "A **homogeneous mixture** looks the same throughout, like salt water, air or vinegar. A **heterogeneous mixture** has visibly different parts, like cereal in milk, sand in water or a pizza."],
  ["how do you separate mixtures | ways to separate a mixture", "Mixtures can be separated by physical methods: **filtering** (sand from water), **evaporation** (salt from water), **distillation** (liquids with different boiling points), **magnets** (iron from sand), **chromatography** (inks) and sieving."],
  ["what is distillation | how does distillation work", "**Distillation** separates liquids by boiling the mixture so the part with the lower boiling point turns to vapor first; the vapor is then cooled and condensed back into a liquid in a separate container. It's used to make pure water from salt water."],
  ["what is chromatography | how does paper chromatography work", "**Chromatography** separates a mixture by letting a solvent carry it through a material like paper. Different substances travel at different speeds, so a single dot of black ink can spread into bands of colors."],
  ["what is a solute and solvent | what is the universal solvent", "In a solution, the **solute** is what gets dissolved (like sugar), and the **solvent** does the dissolving (like water). Water is called the **universal solvent** because it dissolves more substances than any other liquid."],
  ["what is a saturated solution | what is solubility", "**Solubility** is how much of a solute can dissolve in a solvent. A **saturated** solution has dissolved as much as it can at that temperature; any extra just sits at the bottom. Most solids dissolve better in hotter water."],
  ["what is concentration | concentrated vs dilute", "**Concentration** is how much solute is in a given amount of solution. A **concentrated** solution has a lot of solute (strong juice), while a **dilute** solution has little (watery juice)."],
  ["what is a suspension | what is a colloid", "A **suspension** has particles big enough to settle out over time, like muddy water. A **colloid** has tiny particles that stay spread out and don't settle, like milk, fog, gelatin and whipped cream."],
  ["what are examples of acids | common acids at home", "Common acids include **lemon juice** (citric acid), **vinegar** (acetic acid), soda (carbonic and phosphoric acid), and your **stomach acid** (hydrochloric acid). Acids taste sour, but never taste unknown chemicals!"],
  ["what are examples of bases | common bases at home", "Common bases (alkalis) include **baking soda**, **soap**, ammonia cleaners, toothpaste, antacid tablets and bleach. Bases taste bitter and feel slippery. Strong bases like drain cleaner are very dangerous."],
  ["what is a neutral ph | what is the ph of water", "A pH of **7** is neutral, neither acidic nor basic. Pure water has a pH of 7; anything below 7 is acidic and anything above 7 is basic (alkaline)."],
  ["what is the ph of common things | ph of lemon juice and bleach", "Approximate pH values: battery acid about 0 to 1, stomach acid 1 to 2, lemon juice about 2, vinegar about 2.5, coffee about 5, pure water 7, blood about 7.4, baking soda about 8.5, ammonia about 11 and bleach about 12 to 13."],
  ["what is an indicator in chemistry | what is litmus paper", "An **indicator** changes color depending on pH. **Litmus paper** turns red in acid and blue in base. Universal indicator shows a whole rainbow of colors across the pH scale."],
  ["how do you make a red cabbage ph indicator | red cabbage indicator", "Chop red cabbage, soak it in hot water for about 10 minutes and strain it to get purple juice. Add a little to different liquids: it turns **pink or red** in acids like vinegar, stays purple when neutral, and turns **green or yellow** in bases like baking soda solution."],
  ["what is neutralization | what happens when you mix an acid and a base", "In **neutralization**, an acid and a base react to cancel each other out, forming a **salt and water**. For example, hydrochloric acid + sodium hydroxide makes sodium chloride + water. Antacids neutralize extra stomach acid."],
  ["strong acid vs weak acid | what makes an acid strong", "A **strong acid**, like hydrochloric acid, breaks apart completely in water, releasing lots of hydrogen ions. A **weak acid**, like vinegar or citric acid, only partly breaks apart. Strength isn't the same as concentration."],
  ["what are signs of a chemical change | how do you know a chemical reaction happened", "Signs of a chemical change include a **color change**, **gas bubbles**, a **new smell**, **temperature change**, **light** being given off, or a solid (**precipitate**) forming when two liquids mix."],
  ["what are examples of physical changes | is melting a physical change", "Physical changes alter a substance's form but not what it is. Examples include **melting**, freezing, boiling, cutting, crushing, bending and dissolving sugar in water. Melting ice is still water, so it's a physical change."],
  ["what are examples of chemical changes | is burning a chemical change", "Chemical changes make new substances. Examples include **burning** wood, **rusting** iron, **baking** a cake, frying an egg, fruit ripening, digesting food and milk going sour."],
  ["what are reactants and products | what does the arrow mean in a chemical equation", "In a chemical equation, **reactants** are the starting substances on the left, and **products** are the new substances formed on the right. The arrow means \"react to make\" or \"yields.\""],
  ["what is balancing chemical equations | why do we balance equations", "**Balancing** an equation means making sure the same number of each type of atom is on both sides, because atoms can't be created or destroyed. You only change the big numbers in front (coefficients), never the small subscripts."],
  ["how do you balance a chemical equation example | balance h2 + o2 = h2o", "Take H2 + O2 -> H2O: there are 2 oxygens on the left but 1 on the right. Put a 2 before H2O, giving 4 hydrogens on the right, then a 2 before H2: **2H2 + O2 -> 2H2O**. Now each side has 4 H and 2 O."],
  ["what is the law of conservation of mass | is mass lost in a chemical reaction", "The **law of conservation of mass** says mass is neither created nor destroyed in a chemical reaction. The total mass of the products equals the total mass of the reactants; atoms are just rearranged."],
  ["who discovered conservation of mass | who is the father of modern chemistry", "**Antoine Lavoisier**, an 18th-century French chemist, showed through careful weighing that mass is conserved in chemical reactions. He's often called the father of modern chemistry and named oxygen and hydrogen."],
  ["what are the types of chemical reactions | five types of reactions", "The five main types are **synthesis** (A + B -> AB), **decomposition** (AB -> A + B), **single replacement** (A + BC -> AC + B), **double replacement** (AB + CD -> AD + CB) and **combustion** (fuel + oxygen -> CO2 + water)."],
  ["what is a synthesis reaction | example of a synthesis reaction", "In a **synthesis reaction**, two or more substances combine to form one product. Example: iron + sulfur -> iron sulfide, or 2Na + Cl2 -> 2NaCl (table salt)."],
  ["what is a decomposition reaction | example of a decomposition reaction", "In a **decomposition reaction**, one compound breaks down into simpler substances. Example: passing electricity through water splits it into hydrogen and oxygen gas (2H2O -> 2H2 + O2)."],
  ["what is a combustion reaction | what is burning chemically", "**Combustion** is a fast reaction between a fuel and **oxygen** that releases heat and light. When fuels made of carbon and hydrogen burn completely, they produce carbon dioxide and water."],
  ["what is the fire triangle | what does fire need to burn", "Fire needs three things: **heat**, **fuel** and **oxygen**, called the fire triangle. Take away any one and the fire goes out, which is how firefighters and fire extinguishers work."],
  ["what is a single replacement reaction | what is a double replacement reaction", "In **single replacement**, one element replaces another in a compound, like zinc + copper sulfate -> zinc sulfate + copper. In **double replacement**, two compounds swap partners, often forming a precipitate or water."],
  ["exothermic vs endothermic reactions | what is an exothermic reaction", "An **exothermic** reaction releases heat, so it feels warm, like burning or hand warmers. An **endothermic** reaction absorbs heat, so it feels cold, like instant cold packs or baking soda dissolving in vinegar."],
  ["what affects the rate of a reaction | how to speed up a chemical reaction", "Reactions go faster with higher **temperature**, higher **concentration**, greater **surface area** (smaller pieces) and a **catalyst**. All of these make particles collide more often or more effectively."],
  ["what is a precipitate | what is precipitation in chemistry", "A **precipitate** is a solid that forms when two solutions are mixed and react. For example, mixing silver nitrate and salt water instantly makes cloudy white silver chloride."],
  ["what is oxidation | what is a redox reaction", "**Oxidation** is when a substance loses electrons, often by combining with oxygen, like iron rusting. **Reduction** is gaining electrons. They always happen together, so these are called **redox** reactions."],
  ["why do apples turn brown | why does a cut apple go brown", "When you cut an apple, an enzyme in it reacts with **oxygen** in the air, making brown compounds. Lemon juice slows it down because its acid and vitamin C interfere with that reaction."],
  ["what is a mole in chemistry | what is avogadro's number", "A **mole** is a counting unit for tiny particles, like a \"dozen\" but huge: **6.022 × 10²³** particles, called Avogadro's number. One mole of carbon-12 atoms has a mass of exactly 12 grams."],
  ["what is organic chemistry | what are organic compounds", "**Organic chemistry** studies compounds containing **carbon**, usually bonded to hydrogen. Living things are built from organic compounds, and so are plastics, fuels and many medicines."],
  ["what are hydrocarbons | examples of hydrocarbons", "**Hydrocarbons** are compounds made only of hydrogen and carbon. Examples include methane (natural gas), propane, butane and octane in gasoline. They're the main ingredients of fossil fuels."],
  ["what are polymers | what is plastic made of", "**Polymers** are huge molecules made of many repeating small units called monomers. Plastics like polyethylene are synthetic polymers, while natural polymers include starch, cellulose, proteins, rubber and DNA."],
  ["what are the properties of solids liquids and gases | particles in solids liquids and gases", "In a **solid**, particles are packed tightly and vibrate in place, so it has a fixed shape and volume. In a **liquid**, particles are close but slide past each other, so it has a fixed volume but takes the container's shape. In a **gas**, particles are far apart and move fast, filling any container."],
  ["what is melting point | what is boiling point | melting point vs boiling point", "The **melting point** is the temperature where a solid turns into a liquid, and the **boiling point** is where a liquid turns into a gas. For water at sea level, these are 0 degrees C (32 F) and 100 degrees C (212 F)."],
  ["what is sublimation | what is deposition in chemistry", "**Sublimation** is when a solid turns directly into a gas without becoming a liquid, like dry ice. **Deposition** is the reverse, when gas turns straight into a solid, like frost forming on a cold window."],
  ["why do cold glasses get wet | why does water form on the outside of a cold drink", "**Condensation** is when a gas cools and turns into a liquid. Water vapor in the air touches a cold glass, cools down and condenses into droplets on the outside."],
  ["evaporation vs boiling | difference between evaporation and boiling", "**Evaporation** is when a liquid turns into gas at its surface, at any temperature, like a puddle drying up. **Boiling** happens throughout the liquid at a specific temperature, forming bubbles inside it."],
  ["what is the kinetic theory of matter | why do things expand when heated", "The **kinetic theory** says all matter is made of tiny particles in constant motion, and heating makes them move faster. Faster-moving particles push farther apart, which is why most things **expand** when heated and shrink when cooled."],
  ["does water expand when it freezes | why do pipes burst in winter", "Yes, water expands by about **9%** when it freezes, because the molecules lock into an open, hexagonal crystal pattern. That expansion can crack rocks and burst water pipes in freezing weather."],
  ["what is a bose einstein condensate | is there a fifth state of matter", "A **Bose-Einstein condensate** is a state of matter made when certain atoms are cooled to nearly **absolute zero**. The atoms clump together and act like one giant \"super atom.\" It was first made in a lab in 1995."],
  ["density formula | density equals mass over volume | density example problem", "Density = **mass / volume** (D = m/V). For example, a block with a mass of 200 g and a volume of 100 cm³ has a density of 2 g/cm³. Water's density is about 1 g/cm³."],
  ["what is volume | how do you measure volume", "**Volume** is how much space something takes up. For a box, multiply length x width x height; for liquids, use a measuring cylinder; for odd shapes, drop them in water and measure how much the water rises (displacement)."],
  ["what is a chemical property vs physical property | examples of physical properties", "**Physical properties** can be observed without changing the substance, like color, density, melting point and hardness. **Chemical properties** describe how it reacts, like flammability or whether it rusts."],
  ["what is an alloy | examples of alloys", "An **alloy** is a mixture of a metal with other elements to improve its properties. Examples include **steel** (iron + carbon), **bronze** (copper + tin), **brass** (copper + zinc) and **stainless steel** (iron + chromium + nickel)."],
  ["hydrogen element | what is hydrogen used for | symbol for hydrogen", "**Hydrogen (H)**, atomic number 1, is the lightest and most abundant element in the universe. It powers the stars through fusion, is part of every water molecule, and is used to make ammonia for fertilizer and as a clean fuel."],
  ["helium element | what is helium used for | symbol for helium", "**Helium (He)**, atomic number 2, is a light, unreactive noble gas. It fills party balloons and blimps, cools the superconducting magnets in MRI machines, and was first detected in the Sun's light before it was found on Earth."],
  ["lithium element | what is lithium used for | symbol for lithium", "**Lithium (Li)**, atomic number 3, is the lightest metal; it's so soft you can cut it with a knife and light enough to float on oil. It's the key ingredient in rechargeable **lithium-ion batteries** in phones, laptops and electric cars."],
  ["beryllium element | what is beryllium used for", "**Beryllium (Be)**, atomic number 4, is a light, strong, toxic metal. It's used in aerospace parts and in the mirrors of the James Webb Space Telescope, and emeralds are a form of the beryllium mineral beryl."],
  ["boron element | what is boron used for", "**Boron (B)**, atomic number 5, is a metalloid. It's used in heat-resistant borosilicate glass (like lab glassware), in borax cleaning products, and is an important nutrient for plants."],
  ["carbon element facts | what is carbon used for | symbol for carbon", "**Carbon (C)**, atomic number 6, is the basis of all known life because it can form four bonds and long chains. Pure carbon comes as diamond, graphite (pencil \"lead\") and graphene, and it's used in steel and fuels."],
  ["nitrogen element | what is nitrogen used for | symbol for nitrogen", "**Nitrogen (N)**, atomic number 7, makes up about **78%** of the air. It's essential for proteins and DNA, is used in fertilizers, and super-cold liquid nitrogen (-196 degrees C) is used to freeze things instantly."],
  ["oxygen element facts | what is oxygen used for | symbol for oxygen", "**Oxygen (O)**, atomic number 8, makes up about 21% of air and is the most common element in Earth's crust. Living things use it to release energy from food, fires need it to burn, and hospitals give it to patients who need help breathing."],
  ["fluorine element | what is fluorine used for | why is fluoride in toothpaste", "**Fluorine (F)**, atomic number 9, is the most reactive element of all. As **fluoride** in toothpaste and tap water, it helps strengthen tooth enamel and prevent cavities; it's also used in nonstick pan coatings."],
  ["neon element | what is neon used for | symbol for neon", "**Neon (Ne)**, atomic number 10, is a noble gas that glows bright **reddish-orange** when electricity passes through it, which is why it's used in neon signs. Other \"neon\" sign colors come from different gases or coatings."],
  ["sodium element | what is sodium used for | symbol for sodium", "**Sodium (Na)**, atomic number 11, is a soft, very reactive alkali metal that fizzes violently in water. Combined with chlorine it makes table salt, and your nerves need sodium ions to send signals. Its symbol comes from the Latin *natrium*."],
  ["magnesium element | what is magnesium used for | symbol for magnesium", "**Magnesium (Mg)**, atomic number 12, is a light metal that burns with a dazzling white flame, used in fireworks and flares. It makes strong, light alloys for cars and laptops, and it sits at the center of every chlorophyll molecule."],
  ["aluminum element | aluminium | what is aluminum used for | symbol for aluminum", "**Aluminum (Al)**, atomic number 13, is the most abundant metal in Earth's crust. It's light, doesn't rust, and is easy to recycle, so it's used for drink cans, foil, airplanes, window frames and power lines."],
  ["silicon element | what is silicon used for | symbol for silicon", "**Silicon (Si)**, atomic number 14, is a metalloid and the second most common element in Earth's crust (sand is silicon dioxide). It's a **semiconductor**, making it the heart of computer chips and solar cells, which is how Silicon Valley got its name."],
  ["phosphorus element | what is phosphorus used for", "**Phosphorus (P)**, atomic number 15, is essential for life: it's in DNA, ATP and your bones and teeth. It's a key part of fertilizers, and red phosphorus is used on the striking strip of matchboxes."],
  ["sulfur element | what is sulfur used for | why does sulfur smell like eggs", "**Sulfur (S)**, atomic number 16, is a yellow nonmetal found near volcanoes. It's used to make sulfuric acid, rubber and fertilizers. Pure sulfur barely smells; the rotten-egg smell comes from compounds like hydrogen sulfide."],
  ["chlorine element | what is chlorine used for | symbol for chlorine", "**Chlorine (Cl)**, atomic number 17, is a toxic greenish-yellow gas, a halogen. In small controlled amounts it disinfects swimming pools and drinking water, and it's in bleach, PVC plastic, and (as chloride) in table salt."],
  ["argon element | what is argon used for", "**Argon (Ar)**, atomic number 18, is a noble gas making up almost **1%** of air. Because it doesn't react, it fills light bulbs, shields metal during welding and is put between double-glazed windows for insulation."],
  ["potassium element | what is potassium used for | symbol for potassium", "**Potassium (K)**, atomic number 19, is a soft, highly reactive alkali metal that bursts into lilac flames in water. Your nerves and muscles need it, bananas are rich in it, and it's a main ingredient in fertilizer. K comes from the Latin *kalium*."],
  ["calcium element | what is calcium used for | symbol for calcium", "**Calcium (Ca)**, atomic number 20, is a metal that builds strong **bones and teeth** and helps muscles contract. It's found in milk, chalk, limestone, eggshells and seashells (as calcium carbonate), and is used to make cement."],
  ["titanium element | what is titanium used for | symbol for titanium", "**Titanium (Ti)**, atomic number 22, is as strong as many steels but much lighter, and it doesn't corrode. It's used in airplanes, spacecraft, artificial hip joints and dental implants, and titanium dioxide is a bright white pigment in paint and sunscreen."],
  ["chromium element | what is chromium used for", "**Chromium (Cr)**, atomic number 24, is a hard, shiny metal. It's mixed into **stainless steel** to stop rusting and used for chrome plating on cars and taps. Rubies get their red color from traces of chromium."],
  ["manganese element | what is manganese used for", "**Manganese (Mn)**, atomic number 25, is a hard, brittle metal mostly used to make steel stronger. It's also in alkaline batteries and is a nutrient your body needs in small amounts."],
  ["iron element | what is iron used for | symbol for iron", "**Iron (Fe)**, atomic number 26, is the most used metal on Earth, mainly to make **steel** for buildings, cars and tools. Your blood's hemoglobin uses iron to carry oxygen, and Earth's core is mostly iron. Fe comes from the Latin *ferrum*."],
  ["cobalt element | what is cobalt used for", "**Cobalt (Co)**, atomic number 27, is a hard, magnetic metal used in rechargeable batteries, strong magnets and jet engine alloys. Cobalt compounds create a deep \"cobalt blue\" color in glass and pottery, and vitamin B12 contains cobalt."],
  ["nickel element | what is nickel used for | symbol for nickel", "**Nickel (Ni)**, atomic number 28, is a silvery, magnetic metal that resists corrosion. It's used in stainless steel, rechargeable batteries and coins. The US 5-cent \"nickel\" is actually 75% copper and only 25% nickel."],
  ["copper element | what is copper used for | symbol for copper", "**Copper (Cu)**, atomic number 29, is a reddish metal that's an excellent conductor of electricity, so it's used in **wires**, motors and electronics, plus water pipes and coins. Exposed copper slowly turns green, which is why the Statue of Liberty is green. Cu comes from Latin *cuprum*."],
  ["zinc element | what is zinc used for | symbol for zinc", "**Zinc (Zn)**, atomic number 30, is used to coat steel so it won't rust (galvanizing), in brass, in batteries and in sunscreen (zinc oxide). Your immune system also needs a little zinc to work well."],
  ["gallium element | what is gallium | metal that melts in your hand", "**Gallium (Ga)**, atomic number 31, is a metal that melts at about 30 degrees C (86 F), so it can melt in your hand. It's used in LEDs, lasers and some solar cells."],
  ["germanium element | what is germanium used for", "**Germanium (Ge)**, atomic number 32, is a metalloid and semiconductor. It was used in the first transistors and is now used in fiber-optic cables and infrared camera lenses. Mendeleev predicted it before it was discovered."],
  ["arsenic element | what is arsenic", "**Arsenic (As)**, atomic number 33, is a metalloid famous for being poisonous. Today it's used in small amounts in some semiconductors and alloys, and it's carefully monitored in drinking water."],
  ["bromine element | what is bromine | which element is a liquid at room temperature", "**Bromine (Br)**, atomic number 35, is one of only two elements that are **liquid** at room temperature (the other is mercury). It's a reddish-brown halogen with a strong smell, used in fire retardants and pool chemicals."],
  ["krypton element | is krypton a real element", "Yes! **Krypton (Kr)**, atomic number 36, is a real noble gas, not just Superman's home planet. It's used in some flash lamps, energy-efficient windows and lighting, and its name means \"the hidden one\" in Greek."],
  ["strontium element | what is strontium used for", "**Strontium (Sr)**, atomic number 38, is a soft metal whose compounds burn with a brilliant **red** flame, so it's used to make red fireworks and emergency flares."],
  ["silver element | what is silver used for | symbol for silver", "**Silver (Ag)**, atomic number 47, is the best electrical conductor of all metals. It's used in jewelry, coins, mirrors, electronics and some medical dressings because it kills germs. Ag comes from the Latin *argentum*."],
  ["tin element | what is tin used for | symbol for tin", "**Tin (Sn)**, atomic number 50, is a soft, silvery metal. It's used to coat steel \"tin cans\" so they don't rust, in solder for joining electronics, and mixed with copper to make bronze. Sn comes from the Latin *stannum*."],
  ["iodine element | what is iodine used for", "**Iodine (I)**, atomic number 53, is a shiny purple-black halogen that turns into a violet vapor when heated. Your thyroid gland needs it, so it's added to table salt, and iodine solutions are used to disinfect wounds and to test for starch (it turns blue-black)."],
  ["xenon element | what is xenon used for", "**Xenon (Xe)**, atomic number 54, is a heavy noble gas used in very bright lamps like car headlights, camera flashes and movie projectors. Ion engines on some spacecraft use xenon as propellant."],
  ["cesium element | caesium | what is cesium used for", "**Cesium (Cs)**, atomic number 55, is a very soft, gold-tinted alkali metal that melts at about 28 degrees C and explodes in water. **Atomic clocks** use cesium atoms to keep incredibly accurate time; the second is defined by them."],
  ["barium element | what is barium used for", "**Barium (Ba)**, atomic number 56, is a reactive metal. Its compounds make **green** fireworks, and patients drink a barium sulfate \"milkshake\" so their digestive system shows up clearly on X-rays."],
  ["neodymium element | what are neodymium magnets", "**Neodymium (Nd)**, atomic number 60, is a rare earth metal used to make the **strongest permanent magnets** known. They're found in headphones, hard drives, electric car motors and wind turbines."],
  ["tungsten element | what is tungsten used for | why is tungsten w", "**Tungsten (W)**, atomic number 74, has the **highest melting point** of any metal, about 3,422 degrees C. It's used in old light bulb filaments, drill bits and very hard tools. W comes from its other name, *wolfram*."],
  ["platinum element | what is platinum used for", "**Platinum (Pt)**, atomic number 78, is a rare, heavy, silvery precious metal that doesn't corrode. It's used in jewelry, in car **catalytic converters** to clean exhaust gases, and in lab equipment and some cancer drugs."],
  ["gold element facts | what is gold used for | why doesn't gold rust", "**Gold (Au)**, atomic number 79, hardly reacts with anything, so it never rusts or tarnishes. That makes it great for jewelry, coins and reliable electrical contacts in phones and computers. It's so malleable that one gram can be beaten into a sheet about 1 square meter."],
  ["mercury element | what is mercury used for | symbol for mercury", "**Mercury (Hg)**, atomic number 80, is the only metal that's **liquid** at room temperature. It was once used in thermometers, but it's toxic, so it's being phased out. Hg comes from its Latin name *hydrargyrum*, meaning \"liquid silver.\""],
  ["lead element | what is lead used for | symbol for lead", "**Lead (Pb)**, atomic number 82, is a heavy, soft metal. It's used in car batteries and to shield against X-rays, but it's toxic, so it's no longer used in paint, gasoline or water pipes. Pb comes from the Latin *plumbum*, which also gave us \"plumber.\""],
  ["radon element | what is radon", "**Radon (Rn)**, atomic number 86, is a radioactive noble gas with no color or smell. It seeps out of the ground from decaying uranium and can build up in basements, so many homes are tested for it."],
  ["radium element | who discovered radium", "**Radium (Ra)**, atomic number 88, is a highly radioactive metal discovered by **Marie and Pierre Curie** in 1898. It was once used in glow-in-the-dark watch paint until people learned how dangerous its radiation was."],
  ["uranium element | what is uranium used for | symbol for uranium", "**Uranium (U)**, atomic number 92, is a heavy, radioactive metal. The isotope uranium-235 can split in **nuclear fission**, releasing huge energy, which is used to fuel nuclear power plants."],
  ["plutonium element | what is plutonium", "**Plutonium (Pu)**, atomic number 94, is a radioactive, mostly human-made element. It's used in nuclear reactors and as a long-lasting power source (RTGs) for deep-space probes like Voyager and the Curiosity rover."],
  ["oganesson element | what is the last element on the periodic table | element 118", "**Oganesson (Og)**, element 118, is currently the last element on the periodic table. It's made in labs only a few atoms at a time and lasts less than a millisecond before decaying."],
  ["what elements are liquid at room temperature | liquid elements", "Only **mercury** and **bromine** are liquid at standard room temperature. Gallium and cesium melt just above it, on a warm day or in your hand."],
  ["which elements are gases at room temperature | gaseous elements", "Eleven elements are gases at room temperature: **hydrogen, nitrogen, oxygen, fluorine, chlorine**, and the six noble gases **helium, neon, argon, krypton, xenon and radon**."],
  ["what is the most reactive metal | most reactive element", "**Fluorine** is the most reactive element overall. Among metals, **cesium** and **francium** are the most reactive; in practice cesium is the most reactive metal you'll see, since francium is extremely rare and radioactive."],
  ["what elements are in the human body | what is the human body made of chemically", "By mass, your body is about **65% oxygen**, 18% carbon, 10% hydrogen and 3% nitrogen, plus calcium, phosphorus, potassium, sulfur, sodium, chlorine and magnesium. Most of that oxygen and hydrogen is in water."],
  ["why are some element symbols weird | why is sodium na | elements with latin symbols", "Some symbols come from **Latin** names: Na (natrium, sodium), K (kalium, potassium), Fe (ferrum, iron), Cu (cuprum, copper), Ag (argentum, silver), Sn (stannum, tin), Au (aurum, gold), Pb (plumbum, lead) and Hg (hydrargyrum, mercury). W for tungsten comes from *wolfram*."],
  ["which elements are named after people | elements named after scientists", "Several elements honor scientists: **einsteinium** (Einstein), **curium** (Marie and Pierre Curie), **mendelevium** (Mendeleev), **nobelium** (Alfred Nobel), **rutherfordium** (Rutherford), **bohrium** (Niels Bohr) and **copernicium** (Copernicus)."],
  ["which elements are named after places | elements named after countries", "Many elements are named after places: **americium** (the Americas), **francium** (France), **germanium** (Germany), **polonium** (Poland), **californium** (California) and **ytterbium**, **yttrium**, **terbium** and **erbium** (all from the Swedish village of Ytterby)."],
  ["what gives fireworks their colors | firework colors elements", "Firework colors come from metal compounds: **strontium** makes red, **sodium** yellow-orange, **barium** green, **copper** blue, **calcium** orange, and **magnesium** or aluminum make bright white sparks."],
  ["what is a flame test | flame test colors", "A **flame test** identifies metals by the color they turn a flame. Lithium gives crimson red, sodium bright orange-yellow, potassium lilac, calcium brick red, and copper blue-green."],
  ["what are the most common elements in earth's crust", "Earth's crust is about **46% oxygen** and **28% silicon** by mass, followed by aluminum, iron, calcium, sodium, potassium and magnesium. Most of it is in silicate minerals."],
  ["what are synthetic elements | are some elements man made", "Yes. Elements heavier than uranium (above 92) are mostly **synthetic**, made in nuclear reactors or particle accelerators. Technetium (43) was the first element ever made artificially, in 1937."],
  ["what are noble metals | what are precious metals", "**Precious metals** are rare, valuable metals that resist corrosion, mainly **gold**, **silver**, **platinum** and **palladium**. They're used in jewelry, coins, electronics and as investments."],
  ["what is newton's first law | law of inertia | first law of motion", "**Newton's first law** says an object at rest stays at rest, and an object in motion keeps moving at the same speed in a straight line, unless an unbalanced force acts on it. This is also called the law of inertia."],
  ["what is newton's second law | f equals ma | second law of motion", "**Newton's second law** says force equals mass times acceleration (**F = ma**). Pushing harder makes something speed up faster, and a heavier object needs more force to get the same acceleration."],
  ["what is newton's third law | for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction | third law of motion", "**Newton's third law** says forces come in pairs: when one object pushes on another, the second pushes back with an equal force in the opposite direction. A rocket pushes gas down, and the gas pushes the rocket up."],
  ["examples of newton's third law | third law examples in real life", "Examples: when you swim, you push water backward and it pushes you forward; a gun recoils when it fires; when you jump, you push down on the ground and it pushes you up; a balloon zooms off as air rushes out the back."],
  ["examples of inertia | inertia in everyday life", "**Inertia** is an object's tendency to resist changes in its motion. The more mass, the more inertia. It's why you lurch forward when a car brakes suddenly, and why a tablecloth can be yanked out from under dishes."],
  ["why do we wear seatbelts physics | seatbelts and inertia", "When a car stops suddenly, your body keeps moving forward because of **inertia**. A seatbelt applies a force to stop you safely with the car instead of letting you hit the dashboard or windshield."],
  ["how do you calculate speed | speed formula | speed distance time", "**Speed = distance / time**. If you cycle 30 km in 2 hours, your average speed is 15 km/h. Rearranged, distance = speed x time and time = distance / speed."],
  ["how do you calculate acceleration | acceleration formula", "**Acceleration** is how quickly velocity changes: acceleration = change in velocity / time. Speeding up, slowing down (deceleration) and changing direction are all forms of acceleration. It's measured in m/s squared."],
  ["what is a newton unit | what is force measured in", "Force is measured in **newtons (N)**, named after Isaac Newton. One newton is the force needed to accelerate a 1 kg mass by 1 m/s squared, roughly the weight of a small apple."],
  ["what are balanced and unbalanced forces | balanced vs unbalanced forces", "**Balanced forces** are equal in size and opposite in direction, so they cancel out and motion doesn't change. **Unbalanced forces** cause a change in motion, making an object speed up, slow down or change direction."],
  ["what is net force | how do you find net force", "**Net force** is the overall force on an object after combining all the forces. Forces in the same direction add up; forces in opposite directions subtract. If net force is zero, the forces are balanced."],
  ["what are contact and non contact forces | types of forces", "**Contact forces** need objects to touch, like friction, tension, air resistance and pushes. **Non-contact forces** act at a distance, like gravity, magnetism and electrostatic force."],
  ["what causes friction | why is there friction", "**Friction** is a force that resists motion when two surfaces rub together. It's caused by tiny bumps on surfaces catching on each other. Rougher surfaces and pushing them together harder both increase friction."],
  ["is friction good or bad | examples of useful friction", "Both! Friction is **useful** for walking without slipping, car brakes, gripping a pencil and lighting matches. It's **unhelpful** when it wears out parts, wastes energy as heat and slows machines, which is why we use oil and ball bearings."],
  ["static vs kinetic friction | types of friction", "**Static friction** stops a resting object from starting to move, and it's usually the strongest. **Kinetic (sliding) friction** acts once it's moving. There's also **rolling friction**, which is much smaller, which is why wheels are so useful."],
  ["what is air resistance | what is drag", "**Air resistance** (drag) is friction from air pushing against a moving object. It increases with speed and surface area, which is why parachutes are big and race cars are streamlined."],
  ["what is terminal velocity | how fast does a skydiver fall", "**Terminal velocity** is the top speed of a falling object, reached when air resistance becomes equal to its weight. A skydiver falling belly-down reaches about 200 km/h (about 120 mph); opening the parachute cuts that to a safe speed."],
  ["what is tension force | what is normal force", "**Tension** is the pulling force in a rope, cable or string that's being pulled tight. The **normal force** is the support force a surface pushes back with, like a table holding up a book."],
  ["how do you calculate momentum | momentum formula", "**Momentum** is mass in motion: momentum = **mass x velocity** (p = mv). A heavy truck or a fast bullet has lots of momentum, making it hard to stop."],
  ["what is conservation of momentum | is momentum conserved in a collision", "In a closed system, total **momentum is conserved**: the momentum before a collision equals the momentum after. That's why in Newton's cradle, one ball hitting one end sends one ball flying out the other."],
  ["what is impulse | why do airbags work", "**Impulse** is force multiplied by the time it acts, and it equals the change in momentum. Airbags, crumple zones and bending your knees when landing make a stop take **longer**, which reduces the force on your body."],
  ["what is circular motion | what is centripetal force", "An object moving in a circle constantly changes direction, so it needs a force pulling it toward the center, called the **centripetal force**. For the Moon it's gravity; for a ball on a string, it's the tension."],
  ["is centrifugal force real | why do you feel pushed outward on a merry go round", "The outward push you feel is really **inertia**: your body wants to keep going straight while the ride pulls you in a circle. \"Centrifugal force\" is a useful idea in a spinning frame, but no real outward force acts on you."],
  ["what is a joule | what is energy measured in", "Energy and work are measured in **joules (J)**. One joule is the work done by a force of 1 newton moving something 1 meter. Lifting an apple 1 meter takes about 1 joule."],
  ["what are the different types of energy | forms of energy list", "Forms of energy include **kinetic** (motion), **gravitational potential**, **elastic potential**, **chemical**, **thermal** (heat), **electrical**, **light** (radiant), **sound** and **nuclear**. Energy constantly changes from one form to another."],
  ["what is chemical energy | examples of chemical energy", "**Chemical energy** is stored in the bonds of molecules and released during chemical reactions. Food, batteries, wood, gasoline and natural gas all store chemical energy."],
  ["what is thermal energy | what is mechanical energy", "**Thermal energy** is the total kinetic energy of the moving particles in a substance; hotter objects have more. **Mechanical energy** is the sum of an object's kinetic and potential energy, like a swinging pendulum."],
  ["what is elastic potential energy | energy in a stretched spring", "**Elastic potential energy** is stored when something is stretched or squashed, like a rubber band, spring, bow or trampoline. It's released when the object springs back to shape."],
  ["what are energy transformations | examples of energy changing form", "Energy constantly changes form. A toaster turns electrical energy into heat, a battery turns chemical into electrical energy, a solar panel turns light into electrical, and a falling ball turns potential into kinetic energy."],
  ["how do you calculate kinetic energy | kinetic energy formula", "Kinetic energy = **1/2 x mass x velocity squared** (KE = ½mv²). Because speed is squared, doubling speed gives **four times** the kinetic energy, which is why fast crashes are so much more dangerous."],
  ["how do you calculate potential energy | gravitational potential energy formula", "Gravitational potential energy = **mass x gravity x height** (PE = mgh). On Earth g is about 9.8 N/kg, so a 2 kg book lifted 1 m gains about 19.6 joules."],
  ["how do you calculate efficiency | efficiency formula physics", "**Efficiency** is how much of the energy put in becomes useful output: efficiency = useful energy out / total energy in x 100%. LED bulbs are more efficient than old filament bulbs because they waste less energy as heat."],
  ["how do you calculate power | power formula", "**Power = work (or energy) / time**, measured in **watts**, where 1 watt = 1 joule per second. A 60-watt bulb uses 60 joules of energy every second."],
  ["what is horsepower | how many watts is a horsepower", "**Horsepower** is an old unit of power invented by James Watt to compare steam engines to horses. One mechanical horsepower is about **746 watts**."],
  ["what is a lever | three classes of levers", "A **lever** is a bar that turns on a pivot called a fulcrum. In a **first-class** lever the fulcrum is in the middle (seesaw, scissors), in **second-class** the load is in the middle (wheelbarrow), and in **third-class** the effort is in the middle (tweezers, your forearm)."],
  ["what is a pulley | how do pulleys make lifting easier", "A **pulley** is a wheel with a rope around it. A single fixed pulley changes the direction of the force, so you pull down to lift up. Combining several pulleys lets you lift heavy loads with less force by pulling more rope."],
  ["what is an inclined plane | how does a ramp make work easier", "An **inclined plane** is a ramp. Pushing a load up a gentle slope takes less force than lifting it straight up, but you have to move it over a longer distance."],
  ["what is a wedge | what is a screw simple machine", "A **wedge** is two inclined planes back to back that split or lift things, like an axe, knife or doorstop. A **screw** is an inclined plane wrapped around a cylinder, turning a twisting force into a strong pushing force."],
  ["what is a wheel and axle | examples of wheel and axle", "A **wheel and axle** is a large wheel fixed to a smaller rod so they turn together. Turning the big wheel takes less force to turn the axle; examples include doorknobs, steering wheels and screwdrivers."],
  ["what is mechanical advantage | do simple machines reduce work", "**Mechanical advantage** is how much a machine multiplies your force. Simple machines don't reduce the total work; they trade force for distance, so you push with less force over a longer distance."],
  ["what is a wave | what do waves carry", "A **wave** is a disturbance that carries **energy** from one place to another without carrying matter along with it. Ocean waves, sound and light are all waves."],
  ["transverse vs longitudinal waves | what is a transverse wave", "In a **transverse wave**, particles move up and down at right angles to the wave's direction, like light or a wiggled rope. In a **longitudinal wave**, particles move back and forth along the wave's direction, like sound, making compressions and rarefactions."],
  ["what are the parts of a wave | what is amplitude and wavelength", "A wave has **crests** (high points) and **troughs** (low points). The **wavelength** is the distance from one crest to the next, and the **amplitude** is the height from the middle to a crest; bigger amplitude means more energy."],
  ["what is frequency of a wave | what is hertz", "**Frequency** is how many waves pass a point each second, measured in **hertz (Hz)**. One hertz is one wave per second; a radio station at 100 MHz broadcasts 100 million waves every second."],
  ["what is the wave equation | wave speed formula", "Wave speed = **frequency x wavelength** (v = f × λ). If the speed stays the same, a higher frequency means a shorter wavelength."],
  ["what is a mechanical wave | electromagnetic vs mechanical waves", "**Mechanical waves**, like sound and water waves, need a medium (solid, liquid or gas) to travel through. **Electromagnetic waves**, like light and radio, can travel through empty space."],
  ["does sound travel faster in water or air | speed of sound in solids", "Sound travels faster in denser, stiffer materials. It goes about **343 m/s** in air, about **1,480 m/s** in water, and around **5,000 m/s** or more in steel, because the particles are closer together and pass vibrations along quickly."],
  ["how does echolocation work | what is sonar", "An **echo** is sound reflecting off a surface and coming back to you. **Echolocation** is when bats, dolphins and some whales make sounds and listen for echoes to find objects and prey; sonar works the same way."],
  ["what is the doppler effect | why does a siren change pitch", "The **Doppler effect** is the change in pitch of a sound when its source moves. As an ambulance approaches, the sound waves bunch up and the pitch is higher; as it moves away, they stretch out and the pitch drops."],
  ["what is infrasound | what animals use infrasound", "Humans can hear roughly **20 Hz to 20,000 Hz**, and the top limit drops with age. Sound below 20 Hz is **infrasound** (elephants and whales use it), and above 20,000 Hz is **ultrasound** (bats use it, and dogs can hear some of it)."],
  ["what is resonance | can a singer break a glass", "**Resonance** happens when something is pushed at its natural frequency, making it vibrate with bigger and bigger amplitude. A trained singer hitting a wine glass's exact natural frequency, loudly enough, can make it shatter."],
  ["how do musical instruments make sound | why do guitar strings make different notes", "Instruments make things **vibrate**: strings, air columns or drum skins. On a guitar, shorter, thinner or tighter strings vibrate faster and make higher notes, and the hollow body amplifies the sound."],
  ["law of reflection | angle of incidence equals angle of reflection", "**Reflection** is when light bounces off a surface. The **law of reflection** says the angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, measured from a line perpendicular to the surface (the normal)."],
  ["why is a mirror image reversed | why are mirrors backwards", "A smooth mirror reflects light in an orderly way, so you see a clear image that appears to be behind the glass. It looks left-right reversed because the mirror actually flips front to back: each point is reflected straight back toward you."],
  ["concave vs convex mirror | what is a concave mirror used for", "A **concave mirror** curves inward like a cave; it can magnify and focus light, so it's used in makeup mirrors, telescopes and flashlights. A **convex mirror** bulges out, giving a wider, smaller view, so it's used in car side mirrors and store security mirrors."],
  ["what is a lens | convex vs concave lens", "A **lens** is a curved piece of transparent material that bends light. A **convex** (converging) lens is thicker in the middle and focuses light to a point, like a magnifying glass. A **concave** (diverging) lens is thinner in the middle and spreads light out."],
  ["what is a focal point | what is focal length", "The **focal point** is where light rays meet after passing through a convex lens or bouncing off a concave mirror. The **focal length** is the distance from the lens to that point; shorter focal lengths mean stronger lenses."],
  ["how do glasses correct vision | nearsighted vs farsighted lenses", "**Nearsighted** people see near things clearly but distant things blurry, so they wear **concave** lenses. **Farsighted** people see far things clearly but near things blurry, so they wear **convex** lenses. Both help focus light onto the retina."],
  ["how does a magnifying glass work | why does a magnifying glass make things bigger", "A magnifying glass is a **convex lens**. When an object is closer than the focal point, the lens bends the light so your eye sees a larger, upright virtual image."],
  ["what is dispersion of light | why does a prism make a rainbow", "A **prism** splits white light into a rainbow because each color bends by a slightly different amount: violet bends the most and red the least. This spreading out is called **dispersion**."],
  ["what are the primary colors of light | rgb color mixing", "The primary colors of **light** are **red, green and blue**. Mixing them adds up: red + green = yellow, red + blue = magenta, green + blue = cyan, and all three make white. Screens use tiny RGB dots this way."],
  ["primary colors of light vs paint | why does mixing paint make brown", "Light mixing is **additive** (more colors = brighter, ending in white). Paint mixing is **subtractive**: each pigment absorbs some colors, so mixing many absorbs nearly everything and you get brown or black. Printers use cyan, magenta, yellow and black."],
  ["transparent translucent and opaque | transparent vs translucent", "**Transparent** materials let light pass straight through so you can see clearly (glass). **Translucent** materials let some light through but blur it (frosted glass, wax paper). **Opaque** materials block light completely (wood, metal)."],
  ["how are shadows formed | why do shadows change size", "A **shadow** forms when an opaque object blocks light, because light travels in straight lines. Moving the object closer to the light source makes the shadow bigger, and shadows are longest when the Sun is low."],
  ["what is total internal reflection | how do fiber optics work", "**Total internal reflection** happens when light inside glass or water hits the surface at a shallow angle and reflects completely back in. **Fiber-optic cables** use it to bounce light along thin glass strands, carrying internet data at high speed."],
  ["what is infrared and ultraviolet | ir vs uv light", "**Infrared** light has longer wavelengths than red; we feel it as heat, and TV remotes and night-vision cameras use it. **Ultraviolet** has shorter wavelengths than violet; it causes sunburn and helps your skin make vitamin D."],
  ["what is an ampere | what is an amp in electricity | how is current measured", "**Electric current** is the flow of electric charge, usually electrons moving through a wire. It's measured in **amperes (amps)**. Current only flows when there's a complete loop, or closed circuit."],
  ["ohm's law example | v equals ir | how to use ohm's law", "**Ohm's law** says **voltage = current x resistance** (V = IR). If a 6-volt battery is connected to a 3-ohm resistor, the current is 6 / 3 = 2 amps."],
  ["examples of electrical insulators | examples of electrical conductors", "**Conductors** let electricity flow easily, like copper, aluminum, gold and salt water. **Insulators** block it, like rubber, plastic, glass, wood and dry air, which is why wires are coated in plastic."],
  ["how do semiconductors work | what is doping in semiconductors", "A **semiconductor**, like silicon, conducts electricity better than an insulator but not as well as a metal. Adding tiny amounts of other elements (doping) lets engineers control its flow precisely, which makes transistors, chips and LEDs possible."],
  ["what is a series circuit | how does a series circuit work", "In a **series circuit**, all components are connected in one single loop, so the same current flows through each. If one bulb breaks, the whole circuit stops, and adding more bulbs makes each one dimmer."],
  ["what is a parallel circuit | how does a parallel circuit work", "In a **parallel circuit**, components are on separate branches, each connected directly to the power source. If one bulb breaks, the others stay lit, and each bulb gets the full voltage. Homes are wired in parallel."],
  ["series vs parallel circuits | difference between series and parallel circuits", "A **series** circuit has one path: the current is the same everywhere, and one break stops everything. A **parallel** circuit has multiple paths: each branch gets full voltage, and one break doesn't stop the others."],
  ["what are circuit symbols | how do you draw a circuit diagram", "Circuit diagrams use simple symbols: a long and short line for a **cell** (battery), a circle with an X for a **bulb**, a rectangle for a **resistor**, a broken line for an open **switch**, and straight lines for **wires**."],
  ["what does a switch do in a circuit | open vs closed circuit", "A **switch** opens or closes a gap in a circuit. When it's **closed**, the circuit is complete and current flows; when it's **open**, there's a gap and current stops."],
  ["what is a short circuit | why are short circuits dangerous", "A **short circuit** is when current takes an unintended path with very little resistance, bypassing the normal load. The current becomes very large, which can overheat wires and start fires, so fuses and circuit breakers cut the power."],
  ["what does a fuse do | what is a circuit breaker", "A **fuse** has a thin wire that melts and breaks the circuit if the current gets too high. A **circuit breaker** does the same job with a switch that trips automatically and can be reset."],
  ["what is a magnetic field | what are magnetic field lines", "A **magnetic field** is the area around a magnet where its force acts. Field lines run from the **north pole** to the **south pole** outside the magnet, and they're closest together where the field is strongest. Iron filings show their pattern."],
  ["what are magnetic poles | do opposite poles attract", "Every magnet has a **north** and a **south pole**. **Opposite poles attract** and **like poles repel**. If you cut a magnet in half, each piece gets its own north and south pole."],
  ["how do you make an electromagnet | homemade electromagnet", "Wrap insulated wire many times around an **iron nail**, then connect the ends to a battery. Current in the coil creates a magnetic field, and the iron makes it stronger, so the nail can pick up paper clips. More coils or more current make it stronger; it gets warm, so disconnect it after use."],
  ["what are electromagnets used for | uses of electromagnets", "Electromagnets can be switched on and off, so they're used in **electric motors**, speakers, doorbells, MRI scanners, maglev trains and the giant cranes that lift scrap metal in junkyards."],
  ["how does an electric motor work | what is a motor", "An **electric motor** turns electrical energy into motion. Current through a coil inside a magnetic field creates forces that push the coil around, making it spin; a part called a commutator keeps it turning in one direction."],
  ["what is electromagnetic induction | how does a generator work", "**Electromagnetic induction** is making electricity by moving a magnet near a coil of wire (or a coil near a magnet). A **generator** spins coils inside magnetic fields to produce current, and Michael Faraday discovered this in 1831."],
  ["what is a transformer | why do power lines use high voltage", "A **transformer** changes the voltage of AC electricity using two coils. Power stations step voltage **up** very high for power lines, because less energy is lost as heat, then transformers step it **down** to safe levels for homes."],
  ["what is conduction | examples of conduction", "**Conduction** is heat moving through direct contact, as fast-vibrating particles bump into their neighbors. A metal spoon in hot soup gets hot along its handle. Metals are good conductors; wood, plastic and air are poor ones."],
  ["what is convection | examples of convection", "**Convection** is heat moving through liquids and gases as warm, less dense fluid rises and cooler, denser fluid sinks, forming **convection currents**. It's how water heats in a pot, how a radiator warms a room and what drives sea breezes."],
  ["what is thermal radiation | how does heat travel through space", "**Radiation** is heat moving as infrared waves, and it needs no material at all, so it can cross empty space. That's how the Sun warms Earth and why you feel warmth from a campfire even with air between you."],
  ["what is a thermal insulator | why do coats keep you warm", "A **thermal insulator** slows heat transfer. Coats, blankets and feathers trap still **air**, which is a poor conductor, so your body heat stays in. Insulation in walls and double-glazed windows work the same way."],
  ["why does metal feel colder than wood | why does metal feel cold at the same temperature", "At the same room temperature, metal **feels** colder because it's a good conductor and draws heat out of your hand quickly. Wood is an insulator, so it pulls heat away slowly and feels warmer."],
  ["how do you convert celsius to kelvin | celsius vs fahrenheit vs kelvin", "To convert Celsius to Fahrenheit, multiply by 9/5 and add 32 (so 20 degrees C = 68 F). To get **Kelvin**, add 273.15 to Celsius. Water freezes at 0 C, 32 F or 273.15 K."],
  ["how does a thermometer work | how does a liquid thermometer work", "A liquid thermometer has alcohol (or, in older ones, mercury) in a thin glass tube. When it warms up, the liquid **expands** and rises up the tube; when it cools, it contracts and drops. Digital thermometers use sensors whose electrical resistance changes with temperature."],
  ["what is specific heat capacity | why does sand get hotter than water", "**Specific heat capacity** is the energy needed to warm 1 kg of a substance by 1 degree. Water has a very high one, so it heats and cools slowly, while sand heats up quickly. That's why a beach's sand gets hot while the sea stays cool."],
  ["pressure formula | pressure equals force over area | why are sharp knives better physics", "**Pressure** is force spread over an area: **pressure = force / area**, measured in pascals. A sharp knife cuts well because its force is concentrated on a tiny area, while snowshoes spread your weight to stop you sinking."],
  ["what is atmospheric pressure | how much does air weigh", "**Air pressure** is the weight of the air above pushing down on everything. At sea level it's about **101,000 pascals**, roughly 1 kg on every square centimeter. It gets lower as you go higher, which is why your ears pop on planes and mountains."],
  ["why does water pressure increase with depth | pressure under water", "Water pressure increases with depth because there's more water above pushing down. Every 10 meters of seawater adds about one atmosphere of pressure, which is why deep-sea submarines need very thick walls."],
  ["how does a straw work | how do suction cups work", "When you suck on a straw, you lower the air pressure inside it, and the higher **air pressure** outside pushes the drink up. A suction cup works the same way: pushing out the air lets the outside air pressure hold it in place."],
  ["what is buoyant force | how does buoyant force work", "**Buoyancy** is the upward force a fluid pushes on an object in it. If the buoyant force is greater than or equal to the object's weight, it floats; if it's less, the object sinks."],
  ["archimedes principle explained | who discovered buoyancy", "**Archimedes' principle** says an object in a fluid is pushed up by a force equal to the **weight of the fluid it displaces**. The ancient Greek scientist Archimedes is said to have realized this while getting into his bath, shouting \"Eureka!\""],
  ["how do submarines dive and surface | how does a submarine float", "Submarines have **ballast tanks**. To dive, they let seawater in, making them heavier than the water they displace. To surface, they blow compressed air into the tanks, pushing water out so they become lighter and rise."],
  ["how do hot air balloons work | why does hot air rise", "Heating air makes its particles spread out, so hot air is **less dense** than the cooler air around it. A balloon full of hot air weighs less than the cool air it displaces, so buoyancy lifts it."],
  ["how do airplanes fly | what is lift", "Airplanes fly because their wings are shaped and tilted to push air **downward**, and the air pushes the wing **up**, creating **lift**. Engines provide **thrust** to overcome **drag**, and lift has to balance the plane's **weight**."],
  ["what is special relativity | special relativity explained", "**Special relativity** (Einstein, 1905) says the speed of light is the same for everyone and nothing with mass can reach it. Its famous equation **E = mc²** means mass and energy are interchangeable, and a small amount of mass holds a huge amount of energy."],
  ["what is general relativity | how does einstein explain gravity", "**General relativity** (Einstein, 1915) describes gravity as massive objects **curving spacetime**, like a bowling ball on a trampoline. Other objects follow those curves, which is why planets orbit stars and light bends around galaxies. GPS satellites must correct for its effects."],
  ["what is spacetime | is time the fourth dimension", "**Spacetime** combines the three dimensions of space with time into one four-dimensional fabric. In relativity, mass and energy warp spacetime, and how fast time passes can depend on your speed and gravity."],
  ["what is the twin paradox | would a space twin age slower", "In the **twin paradox**, one twin travels near light speed and returns home younger than the twin who stayed on Earth. It's a real effect of time dilation, measured with precise clocks on fast airplanes and satellites, though the difference is tiny at everyday speeds."],
  ["what is a photon | is light a particle or a wave", "Light behaves as **both** a wave and a particle; this is called **wave-particle duality**. A **photon** is a single \"packet\" of light energy, and higher-frequency light, like blue or UV, has more energetic photons."],
  ["what is the photoelectric effect | what did einstein win the nobel prize for", "The **photoelectric effect** is when light hitting a metal knocks electrons out of it. Einstein explained it in 1905 by saying light comes in packets (photons), and he won the 1921 **Nobel Prize** for it, not for relativity."],
  ["what is quantum superposition | schrodinger's cat thought experiment", "**Superposition** means a quantum particle can be in a mix of several states at once until it's measured. **Schrodinger's cat** is a thought experiment where a cat in a sealed box seems to be both alive and dead, meant to show how strange this idea is for big objects."],
  ["heisenberg uncertainty principle | explain the uncertainty principle", "The **Heisenberg uncertainty principle** says you can't know both the exact position and the exact momentum of a particle at the same time. The more precisely you know one, the less precisely you can know the other; it's a basic feature of nature, not a problem with our tools."],
  ["what is quantum entanglement | spooky action at a distance", "**Entanglement** is when two particles become linked so that measuring one instantly tells you something about the other, however far apart they are. Einstein called it \"spooky action at a distance,\" but it can't be used to send messages faster than light."],
  ["what is quantum tunneling | can particles go through walls", "**Quantum tunneling** is when a tiny particle passes through a barrier it seemingly shouldn't have enough energy to cross. It's essential for nuclear fusion in the Sun and is used in some electronics and microscopes."],
  ["what is a quantum computer | how are quantum computers different", "A **quantum computer** uses **qubits**, which can use superposition and entanglement instead of being just 0 or 1. For some problems, like simulating molecules or breaking certain codes, they could be far faster than regular computers."],
  ["what are the four fundamental forces | fundamental forces of nature", "The four fundamental forces are **gravity**, **electromagnetism**, the **strong nuclear force** (holds the nucleus together) and the **weak nuclear force** (causes some radioactive decay). The strong force is the strongest, and gravity is by far the weakest."],
  ["what is a scalar and vector | scalar vs vector quantity", "A **scalar** has only size, like mass, speed, temperature or time. A **vector** has both size and direction, like velocity, force, acceleration and displacement."],
  ["what are si units | basic units in physics", "The **SI units** are the international standard. The base units include the **meter** (length), **kilogram** (mass), **second** (time), **ampere** (current), **kelvin** (temperature), **mole** (amount) and **candela** (light intensity)."],
  ["what is a pendulum | what affects a pendulum's swing", "A **pendulum** is a weight swinging on a string or rod. The time for one swing depends mainly on its **length**: longer pendulums swing more slowly. Surprisingly, the weight of the bob barely matters, which made pendulums great for clocks."],
  ["what is projectile motion | why do thrown balls curve", "A thrown ball follows a curved path called a **parabola** because it moves forward at a steady speed while gravity pulls it down. Without air resistance, a ball thrown at **45 degrees** goes the farthest."],
  ["what is free fall | do all objects fall at the same rate without air", "**Free fall** is motion where gravity is the only force acting. Without air resistance, all objects fall at the same rate; astronaut David Scott dropped a hammer and a feather on the Moon in 1971, and they hit the ground together."],
  ["what is center of gravity | why do things tip over", "The **center of gravity** is the point where an object's weight seems to act. An object tips over when its center of gravity moves beyond its base, which is why objects with a low center of gravity and a wide base are more stable."],
  ["what is elasticity | what is hooke's law", "**Elasticity** is a material's ability to return to its shape after being stretched. **Hooke's law** says the stretch of a spring is proportional to the force pulling it, as long as you don't go past its elastic limit."],
  ["what is surface tension | how do water striders walk on water", "**Surface tension** is the \"skin\" on a liquid's surface caused by molecules pulling strongly on each other. It lets water striders walk on ponds, lets a paper clip float carefully on water, and makes water form round droplets."],
  ["what is static charge | why do balloons stick to walls", "Rubbing a balloon on your hair transfers **electrons** to the balloon, giving it a negative charge. Near a wall, it pushes the wall's electrons away slightly, leaving a positive surface that attracts the balloon, so it sticks."],
  ["how does a light bulb work | led vs incandescent", "An **incandescent** bulb passes current through a thin tungsten filament that gets so hot it glows, wasting most energy as heat. An **LED** makes light directly from a semiconductor, using about 75 to 90% less energy and lasting much longer."],
  ["how do solar cells make electricity | photovoltaic cells", "A **solar cell** is made of layers of silicon. When photons of sunlight hit it, they knock electrons loose, and the cell's built-in electric field pushes them into a current. This is called the photovoltaic effect."],
  ["what is entropy | what is the second law of thermodynamics", "The **second law of thermodynamics** says heat naturally flows from hot to cold, and the total **entropy** (disorder, or the spreading out of energy) of an isolated system tends to increase. That's why ice melts in a warm room and machines always waste some energy as heat."],
  ["what is sound energy | how does sound travel through air", "Sound travels as **vibrations** passing from particle to particle. A vibrating object squeezes and stretches the air around it, creating compressions and rarefactions that travel to your ear as a longitudinal wave."]
);
